ons (OR 2.69) all rose (Raymond et al., 2025).", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 6, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q06", "source_urls": []}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "A 62-year-old male with a history of well-controlled schizophrenia and hypertension is scheduled for laparoscopic cholecystectomy. The most appropriate ketamine plan is:", "choice_a": "Bolus 1 mg/kg at induction plus 0.5 mg/kg/hr infusion for 48 h", "choice_b": "Avoid subanesthetic ketamine or use only minimal doses with psychiatric input; document rationale", "choice_c": "Full-dose induction with ketamine to avoid opioids entirely", "choice_d": "Intranasal 1 mg/kg for postoperative breakthrough pain", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Active or unstable psychosis is a relative contraindication. Cautious minimal dosing with psychiatric input is appropriate; large perioperative infusions carry disproportionate psychotomimetic risk (Raymond et al., 2025).", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 7, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q07", "source_urls": []}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "Which adverse effect is most likely to be attenuated by prophylactic glycopyrrolate 0.1\u20130.2 mg IV before ketamine administration?", "choice_a": "Emergence hallucinations", "choice_b": "Tachycardia", "choice_c": "Hypersalivation", "choice_d": "Post-operative nausea", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Ketamine causes muscarinic antagonism and reflex hypersalivation; glycopyrrolate is a targeted antisialagogue (George & Johns, 2020).", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 8, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q08", "source_urls": []}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "For chronic refractory neuropathic pain, current expert-consensus guidance (Cohen et al., 2018; Pickering et al., 2026) recommends ketamine be considered:", "choice_a": "First-line therapy", "choice_b": "A non\u2013first-line option for refractory pain, initiated at ~80 mg over \u22652 