 mg/kg IV with neonatal monitoring if delivery is within 1 h", "choice_b": "3 mg/kg IV induction dose", "choice_c": "Continuous 1 mg/kg/hr infusion for 24 h", "choice_d": "Ketamine is absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Ketamine can be used in obstetric analgesia when other options are contraindicated; subanesthetic doses with neonatal monitoring are appropriate (Duong et al., 2026).", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 12, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q12", "source_urls": []}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "An 82-year-old is scheduled for orthopedic surgery. Compared with a 35-year-old at the same body weight, the CRNA should:", "choice_a": "Increase ketamine dose by 25%", "choice_b": "Reduce ketamine dose by 25\u201350% and titrate slowly", "choice_c": "Use a fixed 100 mg IV bolus regardless of age", "choice_d": "Avoid all opioid-sparing strategies", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Age \u226565 y is a monitoring precaution; lower doses and slower titration reduce dizziness, prolonged emergence, and delirium (George & Johns, 2020).", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 13, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q13", "source_urls": []}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "Continuous end-tidal CO\u2082 monitoring is most strongly indicated during ketamine administration when:", "choice_a": "Ketamine is used alone in a healthy adult", "choice_b": "Ketamine is combined with concurrent opioid or benzodiazepine, or the patient is at high respiratory risk", "choice_c": "Ketamine dose is under 0.1 mg/kg", "choice_d": "The patient is under 40 years old", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Ketamine alone rarely causes clinically important respiratory depression, but co-administered opioids/benzodiazepines or baseline respiratory risk raise the risk (Goordeen et al., 2022).", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 14, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q14", "source_urls": []}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "Which of the following most accurately reflects ketamine's effect on 24-hour postoperative opioid consumption after laparoscopic cholecystectomy (Akram et al., 2026)?", "choice_a": "Increased by ~5 mg morphine equivalents", "choice_b": "Reduced by ~4\u20135 mg morphine equivalents (ketamine subgroup MD \u22125.35)", "choice_c": "No change", "choice_d": "Reduced by 50 mg morphine equivalents", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Meta-analysis MD \u22124.10 mg (overall), ketamine subgroup MD \u22125.35 mg; very-low-certainty evidence (Akram et al., 2026).", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 15, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q15", "source_urls": []}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "According to the SFAR postoperative-pain recommendations, when should an intraoperative ketamine infusion used as an analgesic adjunct be stopped?", "choice_a": "At the moment of extubation", "choice_b": "Approximately 30 minutes before the anticipated end of surgery", "choice_c": "Exactly 60 minutes before extubation in every patient", "choice_d": "Only after discharge from the PACU", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The SFAR recommendations specify stopping the infusion 30 minutes before the end of surgery (Aubrun et al., 2019). This is a source-specific recommendation, not a universal requirement or proof that 30 minutes is superior to 60 minutes for every patient. ASRA/AAPM/ASA acute-pain guidance describes regimens stopped at surgery completion and others continued postoperatively; timing depends on the clinical indication and protocol (Schwenk et al., 2018).", "difficulty": "medium", "source_urls": ["https://sfar.org/wp-content/uploads/2019/10/rfe-revision-of-expert-panel-guidelines-on-postoperative-pain-management.pdf", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6023582/"], "question_number": 16, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q16"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "For emergency-department chemical restraint of an acutely agitated adult, the mean parenteral ketamine dose (Peter et al., 2025) most often used was:", "choice_a": "IM \u2248 3.83 mg/kg or IV \u2248 2.09 mg/kg", "choice_b": "IN 5 mg/kg", "choice_c": "Oral 0.5 mg/kg", "choice_d": "IM 10 mg/kg", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Peter et al. 2025 systematic review: IM mean 3.83 mg/kg; IV mean 2.09 mg/kg; sedation ~6 min; 24.5% required rescue dose.", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 17, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q17", "source_urls": []}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "Which cardiovascular response is a concern when ketamine is administered to a critically ill patient with depleted catecholamine reserves?", "choice_a": "Guaranteed increases in blood pressure and cardiac output", "choice_b": "Direct myocardial depression with possible hemodynamic deterioration", "choice_c": "Selective pulmonary vasodilation without systemic effects", "choice_d": "Complete absence of cardiovascular effects at anesthetic doses", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Ketamine's indirect sympathetic effects usually increase heart rate and blood pressure, but its direct negative inotropic effect may become clinically apparent in catecholamine-depleted patients. Do not assume that ketamine will reliably improve blood pressure or cardiac output in this setting; assess hemodynamics and titrate with appropriate support (Cohen et al., 2018).", "difficulty": "medium", "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6023575/"], "question_number": 18, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q18"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "Which of the following is a MODERATE-certainty finding from Xu et al. (2026) regarding esketamine and ERAS?", "choice_a": "Complete elimination of postoperative pain", "choice_b": "Improvement in postoperative nausea/vomiting and early sleep quality within 72 h", "choice_c": "Reduction of mortality by 50%", "choice_d": "Elimination of postoperative depression", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Xu et al. 2026 rated evidence for esketamine-associated improvement in PONV and early sleep as moderate-certainty; other outcomes remain low or insufficient.", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 19, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q19", "source_urls": []}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "A CRNA reviewing risks with a patient before an outpatient ketamine infusion for CRPS should specifically mention which of the following, per Goordeen et al. (2022)?", "choice_a": "The infusion will cure the pain permanently", "choice_b": "Most side effects are transient (>95%) and resolve after discontinuation", "choice_c": "No side effects have been reported", "choice_d": "The infusion requires no monitoring", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "ASRA News (Goordeen et al., 2022) states >95% of side effects are transient and resolve after discontinuation; monitoring HR, BP, SpO\u2082, LOC, and EtCO\u2082 in high-risk patients is required.", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 20, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q20", "source_urls": []}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "Which of the following is the most accurate statement regarding ketamine and respiratory drive?", "choice_a": "Ketamine reliably eliminates spontaneous respiration and requires immediate mechanical ventilation", "choice_b": "Ketamine typically preserves respiratory drive and often provides bronchodilation", "choice_c": "Ketamine is a potent bronchoconstrictor", "choice_d": "Ketamine reduces functional residual capacity by 50%", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Ketamine is comparatively respiratory-sparing; bronchodilation is a well-recognized clinical feature useful in status asthmaticus (Duong et al., 2026).", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 21, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q21", "source_urls": []}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "A patient with chronic hepatitis is being considered for repeated, high-dose ketamine infusions for refractory pain. Which additional safety measure is most appropriate?", "choice_a": "Omit laboratory monitoring if the first infusion was tolerated", "choice_b": "Use serum ketamine concentrations as the only safety assessment", "choice_c": "Consider baseline and postinfusion liver function tests based on individual risk", "choice_d": "Check liver function only if the patient develops emergence agitation", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The chronic-pain consensus recommends considering baseline and postinfusion liver function tests case by case in patients with baseline liver dysfunction, increased hepatic risk, or anticipated high-dose ketamine exposure at frequent intervals. This is a grade C recommendation with low-certainty evidence, not a requirement for identical testing after every brief perioperative dose (Cohen et al., 2018).", "difficulty": "medium", "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6023575/"], "question_number": 22, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q22"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "Which of the following is TRUE about the ASRA/AAPM/ASA acute pain consensus (Schwenk et al., 2018) as it applies to CRNAs?", "choice_a": "Ketamine is prohibited as a stand-alone acute-pain agent", "choice_b": "Ketamine may be used as a stand-alone acute-pain treatment or as an adjunct to opioids", "choice_c": "Intranasal ketamine is not recognized as a route", "choice_d": "PCA ketamine is contraindicated", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Schwenk et al. 2018 supports ketamine as stand-alone therapy, opioid adjunct, and (to a lesser extent) via the intranasal route, and addresses PCA use.", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 23, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q23", "source_urls": []}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "A patient develops emergence agitation with visual hallucinations after ketamine sedation. The most evidence-supported intervention is:", "choice_a": "Immediate physostigmine 2 mg IV", "choice_b": "Low-dose midazolam (e.g., 0.02 mg/kg IV) plus a quiet recovery environment", "choice_c": "Naloxone 0.4 mg IV", "choice_d": "Flumazenil 0.2 mg IV", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Low-dose midazolam and a quiet emergence environment are the standard interventions for ketamine emergence phenomena; naloxone and flumazenil are not indicated.", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 24, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q24", "source_urls": []}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_1", "stem": "Which statement best captures the current position of ketamine in perioperative pain management, integrating 2024\u20132026 evidence?", "choice_a": "Universal routine ketamine infusion for all abdominal surgery", "choice_b": "A targeted opioid-sparing option whose benefit is real but context-dependent; patient selection, dose, and setting matter more than protocol", "choice_c": "Ketamine should replace all opioids in all surgeries", "choice_d": "Ketamine has been shown to have no perioperative role", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Current evidence, including IMPAKT ERAS negative signal and consistent opioid-sparing meta-analyses, supports context-dependent, patient-selected use rather than routine protocolized administration (Raymond et al., 2025; Xu et al., 2026; Duong et al., 2026).", "difficulty": "medium", "question_number": 25, "question_id": "course_1_module_1_q25", "source_urls": []}]''',
);
