t's standard fasting pathway", "choice_c": "Use a full-liquid diet until two hours before anesthesia", "choice_d": "Ignore dietary preparation when the patient has no nausea", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The Australian/New Zealand recommendations support continuation with a 24-hour clear-fluid diet followed by the stated standard six-hour fast. Apply that guidance in its local protocol context; do not combine it with unrelated fasting rules.", "source_ids": [3], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12420935/"], "learning_objective_ids": [2], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "What conclusion is supported by RESIDUAL's 42% versus 24% increased-RGC findings after at least a seven-day hold?", "choice_a": "A seven-day hold guarantees an empty stomach", "choice_b": "The study proved a statistically significant doubling of aspiration", "choice_c": "Retained contents can persist after a seven-day hold, but this underpowered comparison was statistically inconclusive", "choice_d": "All patients require a hold longer than 21 days", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "RESIDUAL measured gastric contents, not a proven difference in aspiration. Its confidence intervals included no difference and the study was underpowered.", "source_ids": [6], "source_urls": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42265610/"], "learning_objective_ids": [2, 5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "Why should residual gastric content and pulmonary aspiration be reported separately?", "choice_a": "Retained contents are a surrogate risk marker; aspiration is a distinct clinical event", "choice_b": "Every patient with retained fluid will aspirate", "choice_c": "An increased-RGC odds ratio directly equals the absolute aspiration probability", "choice_d": "They are interchangeable names for the same outcome", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The meta-analysis found increased residual contents but did not demonstrate increased aspiration with low-certainty evidence. The outcomes cannot be treated as synonymous.", "source_ids": [4], "source_urls": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40230298/"], "learning_objective_ids": [1, 5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "A patient has recurrent vomiting and cannot tolerate oral intake before an elective procedure. Under SPAQI, what is the appropriate next step?", "choice_a": "Proceed solely because one weekly dose was missed", "choice_b": "Permit a protein shake immediately before anesthesia", "choice_c": "Treat the symptoms as irrelevant if the patient denies reflux", "choice_d": "Refer for prescribing-team diet/medication modification and reassessment before elective anesthesia", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Severe nausea, vomiting, and inability to tolerate intake are significant symptoms in SPAQI's framework. They warrant modification and reassessment rather than reassurance from a fixed hold.", "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/"], "learning_objective_ids": [3, 4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Which statement best describes a reassuring gastric ultrasound assessment?", "choice_a": "One apparently empty antral image proves a completely empty stomach", "choice_b": "An inconclusive image should be recorded as negative", "choice_c": "A technically adequate examination interpreted in context can reduce uncertainty but does not guarantee zero aspiration risk", "choice_d": "A reassuring scan removes all other airway considerations", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Gastric ultrasound has operator, technical, and patient-related limitations. A limited or single-view examination should not be overinterpreted.", "source_ids": [1, 2], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/"], "learning_objective_ids": [4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "A patient is fasting for surgery and normally uses pramlintide with mealtime insulin. Which instruction matches its Medication Guide?", "choice_a": "Do not inject pramlintide when unable to eat or skipping the meal; maintain a separate individualized insulin plan", "choice_b": "Continue pramlintide while fasting because all GLP-1 agents should continue", "choice_c": "Use pramlintide instead of necessary basal insulin", "choice_d": "Double pramlintide to offset surgical stress", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Pramlintide is an amylin analog with meal-dependent dosing, not a GLP-1 agent. It should not be injected when the patient cannot eat, and it does not replace insulin.", "source_ids": [8], "source_urls": ["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=4aea30ff-eb0d-45c1-b114-3127966328ff"], "learning_objective_ids": [1, 4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "Under SPAQI, what solid-food restriction is recommended for a GLP-1 patient without significant GI symptoms?", "choice_a": "No solids for exactly 21.3 hours as a validated safe threshold", "choice_b": "A full-liquid diet without restrictions until induction", "choice_c": "No solids for 24 hours, with clear liquids only during that interval and the specified liquid-fasting cutoffs", "choice_d": "The same two-hour interval for solids and water", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "SPAQI specifies a 24-hour solid fast with clear liquids only, followed by its separate 8-hour or 4-hour liquid intervals. A ROC threshold is not a replacement protocol.", "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/"], "learning_objective_ids": [2, 4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Which entry best supports clinical continuity after an individualized GLP-1 decision?", "choice_a": "Only weekly versus daily dosing, without symptoms or intake times", "choice_b": "The medication and preparation history, risk assessment, chosen mitigation, rationale, and recovery/restart plan", "choice_c": "Only antiemetic orders, without the aspiration-risk assessment", "choice_d": "Only the last-dose date, without the rationale or recovery plan", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Clinically useful documentation communicates what was assessed, why the plan was chosen, and what the next team needs to know. A single isolated medication detail is not a complete clinical handoff.", "source_ids": [1, 2], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/"], "learning_objective_ids": [5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "What does the November 2024 Ozempic aspiration warning emphasize?", "choice_a": "Rare aspiration has been reported during general anesthesia or deep sedation, and patients should disclose use to procedural clinicians", "choice_b": "Aspiration is impossible after standard fasting", "choice_c": "A one-day hold is proven to prevent retained contents", "choice_d": "Only patients using the drug for diabetes require assessment", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The label warns about reported aspiration and notes insufficient data to establish whether modified fasting or temporary interruption reduces retained contents.", "source_ids": [7], "source_urls": ["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/209637s032lbl.pdf"], "learning_objective_ids": [1, 3], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "A GLP-1 patient also uses high-dose opioids and has known gastroparesis. What is the best approach?", "choice_a": "Individualize beyond the routine pathway because additional causes of delayed emptying are present", "choice_b": "Assume SPAQI's routine fasting table guarantees safety", "choice_c": "Ignore the opioids if the GLP-1 drug was held for a week", "choice_d": "Use absence of nausea as proof of normal emptying", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "SPAQI's routine recommendations do not substitute for judgment in patients with other important causes of delayed gastric emptying, including gastroparesis or high-dose opioid use.", "source_ids": [1, 2], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/"], "learning_objective_ids": [3], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "When dexamethasone and ondansetron are selected for adult PONV prophylaxis, which timing is appropriate?", "choice_a": "Both drugs must always be given only after vomiting begins", "choice_b": "Ondansetron at induction and dexamethasone only at discharge", "choice_c": "All GLP-1 patients require three prophylactic agents at induction", "choice_d": "Dexamethasone at induction and ondansetron near the end of surgery", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The cited current PONV summary lists dexamethasone 4\u20138 mg IV at induction and ondansetron 4 mg IV at the end of surgery. Individualize the regimen and contraindications.", "source_ids": [9], "source_urls": ["https://dig.pharmacy.uic.edu/faqs/2026-2/may-2026-faqs/what-are-current-recommendations-for-prevention-and-treatment-of-post-operative-nausea-and-vomiting-ponv/"], "learning_objective_ids": [4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Which finding meets the high-RGC definition used in the cited ultrasound studies?", "choice_a": "Any visible trace of clear gastric fluid", "choice_b": "Only fluid above 5 mL/kg", "choice_c": "An empty antrum on one technically limited view", "choice_d": "Solid content, thick fluid, or estimated clear fluid greater than 1.5 mL/kg", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The research classification includes solids, thick contents, or clear fluid above 1.5 mL/kg. It is not a threshold at which aspiration is certain.", "source_ids": [5, 6], "source_urls": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41032460/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42265610/"], "learning_objective_ids": [3, 4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "Under 2024 multisociety guidance, a stable daily-liraglutide patient has no elevated delayed-emptying risk. What is reasonable?", "choice_a": "A mandatory four-week interruption", "choice_b": "Routine cancellation because the patient took today's dose", "choice_c": "Continuation, using the institutional preparation pathway and individualized assessment", "choice_d": "An automatic rapid-sequence induction based only on daily dosing", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The multisociety guidance permits continuation when elevated risk is absent. The legacy hold schedule is a fallback when shared decision-making specifically favors withholding.", "source_ids": [2], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/"], "learning_objective_ids": [2, 5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Under SPAQI, which liquid-fasting intervals are specified for a GLP-1 patient without significant symptoms?", "choice_a": "2 hours for all liquids, including protein shakes", "choice_b": "4 hours for high-carbohydrate clear liquids and 8 hours for water", "choice_c": "8 hours for clear liquids containing \u226510% glucose and 4 hours for those containing <10%", "choice_d": "24 hours with no fluid intake for every patient", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The specific SPAQI recommendations distinguish high-carbohydrate from no-/low-carbohydrate clear liquids. They do not allow non-clear liquids to be treated as water.", "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/"], "learning_objective_ids": [2, 4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "A time-sensitive case requires general anesthesia and gastric ultrasound shows retained solids. What is the most appropriate planning principle?", "choice_a": "Use deep sedation without an airway rescue plan", "choice_b": "Discuss proceeding versus delay and, if proceeding, use appropriate full-stomach airway management with difficult-airway contingencies", "choice_c": "Ignore the solids because seven days have elapsed since the drug dose", "choice_d": "Administer ondansetron as the sole aspiration-prevention measure", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Confirmed retained contents require a risk-benefit discussion. Multisociety guidance includes considering rapid-sequence induction and tracheal intubation versus cancellation; airway difficulty and urgency still matter.", "source_ids": [2, 3], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12420935/"], "learning_objective_ids": [3, 4, 5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which statement about GLP-1 therapy and perioperative glucose is most accurate?", "choice_a": "GLP-1 drugs stimulate insulin independently of glucose in every patient", "choice_b": "Continuing or stopping therapy never affects perioperative glycemia", "choice_c": "Glucose monitoring is unnecessary during reduced intake", "choice_d": "Glucose-dependent insulin secretion does not remove hypoglycemia risk from concomitant insulin or sulfonylureas, and withholding may worsen glycemia", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Assess the whole medication and nutrition plan. The label notes greater hypoglycemia risk with insulin or insulin secretagogues, and guidance warns about metabolic consequences of withholding.", "source_ids": [1, 2, 7], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/209637s032lbl.pdf"], "learning_objective_ids": [1, 4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "A patient increased a long-acting GLP-1 dose two weeks ago. What does this mean for assessment?", "choice_a": "It is irrelevant if the prescription is for weight management", "choice_b": "It guarantees that aspiration will occur", "choice_c": "It automatically establishes that the stomach is empty", "choice_d": "Escalation is a higher-risk feature that should prompt symptom, intake, preparation, and timing review", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Escalation can increase delayed-emptying and GI effects. It warrants individualized precautions or deferral when appropriate, not a prediction of inevitable aspiration.", "source_ids": [1, 2], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/"], "learning_objective_ids": [3, 5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "When is postoperative resumption of a GLP-1 receptor agonist generally appropriate under SPAQI?", "choice_a": "Immediately after extubation even with persistent vomiting", "choice_b": "Once the usual diet is resumed, with symptoms and any product-specific restart or re-titration needs addressed", "choice_c": "Only after doubling the next dose", "choice_d": "Never again after any anesthetic", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "SPAQI ties resumption to the usual diet. Significant ongoing symptoms and a prolonged interruption require review; product and prescriber instructions govern missed-dose or re-titration decisions.", "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/"], "learning_objective_ids": [4], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_2", "question_id": "course_1_module_2_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Which principle best summarizes perioperative GLP-1 care?", "choice_a": "Use a named guidance pathway with individualized risk assessment, shared decisions, appropriate precautions, and a documented restart plan", "choice_b": "Apply one fixed hold interval without considering symptoms or procedure", "choice_c": "Treat a normal single ultrasound image as a guarantee", "choice_d": "Replace clinical assessment with memorization of ROC cutoffs", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The guidance emphasizes balancing metabolic needs and aspiration concerns. Diet, assessment, anesthetic technique, and follow-up must fit the patient rather than an unvalidated universal interval.", "source_ids": [1, 2, 3], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12597468/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11666732/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12420935/"], "learning_objective_ids": [5], "hint": {"type": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}}]''',
);
final List<dynamic> demoBenzodiazepineQuestions = jsonDecode(
  r'''[{"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Benzodiazepines produce their sedative and anxiolytic effects primarily by:", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Benzodiazepines are positive allosteric modulators. They do not directly open the chloride channel; they increase the frequency of channel opening in the presence of GABA (Peter, Dieudonn\u00e9, & Zolk, 2024).", "source_ids": [1], "learning_objective_ids": [1], "choice_a": "Increasing the frequency of GABA-A chloride channel opening in the presence of endogenous GABA", "choice_b": "Directly opening the GABA-A chloride channel without endogenous GABA", "choice_c": "Antagonizing NMDA receptors in the cortex", "choice_d": "Blocking presynaptic reuptake of glutamate", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["c", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "b"]}, "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.3390/ph17040473"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "Which benzodiazepine is hydrolyzed predominantly by carboxylesterase-1 (CES1), rather than relying on CYP3A metabolism?", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Remimazolam undergoes carboxylesterase-mediated metabolism to inactive CNS7054. Severe hepatic impairment can still reduce clearance; it is not completely organ-independent.", "source_ids": [3, 4], "learning_objective_ids": [2], "choice_a": "Midazolam", "choice_b": "Remimazolam", "choice_c": "Diazepam", "choice_d": "Lorazepam", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["c", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "b"]}, "source_urls": ["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/212295s002lbl.pdf", "https://doi.org/10.1007/s13318-025-00963-2"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "A healthy 45-year-old adult receives midazolam for preoperative anxiolysis. Which IV dosing range is most appropriate as an initial approach?", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "For a healthy adult younger than 60, 1\u20132.5 mg is a reasonable initial titration range, with the initial increment no greater than 2.5 mg over at least 2 minutes and at least 2 additional minutes for reassessment. Lower doses may be needed with co-sedatives.", "source_ids": [2], "learning_objective_ids": [3], "choice_a": "0.05\u20130.1 mg IV once", "choice_b": "5\u201310 mg slow IV push", "choice_c": "1\u20132.5 mg slow IV, titrated with reassessment", "choice_d": "0.5 mg/kg IV bolus", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "c"]}, "source_urls": ["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=94e1342b-f597-44c0-b8fc-b753fde04cbe"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "According to Tsze et al. (2025), which intranasal midazolam doses were identified as optimal for procedural sedation in children undergoing laceration repair?", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The laceration-repair trial retained 0.4 and 0.5 mg/kg intranasal doses (maximum 10 mg) and eliminated 0.2 and 0.3 mg/kg for lower effectiveness. Adequate sedation occurred in approximately 65%\u201370% with the retained doses; this is not a universal dose recommendation.", "source_ids": [6], "learning_objective_ids": [3], "choice_a": "0.1 mg/kg and 0.2 mg/kg", "choice_b": "0.2 mg/kg and 0.3 mg/kg", "choice_c": "0.6 mg/kg and 0.8 mg/kg", "choice_d": "0.4 mg/kg and 0.5 mg/kg", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapediatrics.2025.2181"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "For an 80-year-old debilitated patient requiring IV midazolam premedication, the most appropriate initial approach is:", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "A conservative 0.5\u20131 mg IV starting approach with slow titration and reassessment is appropriate for this vulnerable patient. The injection label limits the geriatric initial increment to 1.5 mg over at least 2 minutes, followed by at least 2 more minutes to assess effect.", "source_ids": [2], "learning_objective_ids": [3, 4], "choice_a": "0.5\u20131 mg IV titrated carefully", "choice_b": "2.5\u20135 mg IV bolus", "choice_c": "0.15 mg/kg IV bolus", "choice_d": "Avoid all benzodiazepines entirely", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["c", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "b"]}, "source_urls": ["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=94e1342b-f597-44c0-b8fc-b753fde04cbe"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "The initial adult flumazenil dose recommended for reversal of procedural sedation is:", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Adult reversal starts with 0.2 mg IV over 15 seconds. If needed, wait an additional 45 seconds before another 0.2 mg; subsequent 0.2-mg doses may be given at 60-second intervals to a maximum cumulative 1 mg.", "source_ids": [5], "learning_objective_ids": [3], "choice_a": "0.05 mg IV over 5 seconds", "choice_b": "0.2 mg IV over 15 seconds", "choice_c": "1 mg IV bolus", "choice_d": "0.01 mg/kg IV over 60 seconds", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["c", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "b"]}, "source_urls": ["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=3ea41cbe-bc07-4c95-8bb2-7dfc2e96a874"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "A child aged 1\u201317 years requires reversal of benzodiazepine-induced conscious sedation. What is the labeled initial IV flumazenil dose?", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Give 0.01 mg/kg IV over 15 seconds, maximum 0.2 mg. Initial titration may continue within labeled intervals and a cumulative limit of 0.05 mg/kg or 1 mg, whichever is lower. Later repeated treatment for pediatric re-sedation is not established.", "source_ids": [5], "learning_objective_ids": [3], "choice_a": "0.05 mg/kg IV bolus", "choice_b": "0.1 mg/kg IV over 60 sec", "choice_c": "0.01 mg/kg IV over 15 sec (maximum 0.2 mg per dose)", "choice_d": "0.5 mg IV fixed dose regardless of weight", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "c"]}, "source_urls": ["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=3ea41cbe-bc07-4c95-8bb2-7dfc2e96a874"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Which of the following is the most important reason to continue monitoring a patient after flumazenil reversal?", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Flumazenil's clinical duration is often shorter than the reversed benzodiazepine \u2014 particularly with long-acting agents or continuous infusions \u2014 so recurrent sedation and respiratory depression may occur. Reversal does not treat coadministered opioids.", "source_ids": [5], "learning_objective_ids": [3, 5], "choice_a": "Flumazenil causes prolonged amnesia beyond the benzodiazepine", "choice_b": "Flumazenil frequently causes malignant hyperthermia", "choice_c": "Flumazenil reverses concurrent opioids", "choice_d": "Flumazenil's duration may be shorter than the benzodiazepine, allowing re-sedation", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "source_urls": ["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=3ea41cbe-bc07-4c95-8bb2-7dfc2e96a874"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "The 2025 APSF Brain Health Patient Safety Priority Advisory Group concluded that in older adults:", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The 2025 APSF Brain Health advisory (Field et al., 2025) states that preoperative doses of short-acting (midazolam) or ultra-short-acting (remimazolam) benzodiazepines need not be proscriptively avoided in older adults, based on the current evidence.", "source_ids": [7], "learning_objective_ids": [5], "choice_a": "Preoperative doses of short- or ultra-short-acting benzodiazepines need not be proscriptively avoided if the goal is to minimize POD", "choice_b": "All benzodiazepines must be permanently avoided perioperatively", "choice_c": "Benzodiazepines eliminate postoperative delirium risk", "choice_d": "Only diazepam is acceptable for premedication", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["c", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "b"]}, "source_urls": ["https://www.apsf.org/wp-content/uploads/newsletters/2025/4003/APSF4003-a07-Perioperative-Brain-Health.pdf"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Xi et al. (2025) pooled 11 studies, including randomized trials and cohorts, with varying control sedatives. Which secondary-outcome finding favored remimazolam?", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The pooled review reported lower odds of hypotension (OR 0.29; 95% CI 0.20\u20130.42) and bradycardia (OR 0.19; 95% CI 0.05\u20130.76), without a statistically significant reduction in postoperative delirium. The evidence was not 11 exclusively propofol-controlled RCTs.", "source_ids": [10], "learning_objective_ids": [2, 5], "choice_a": "Higher risk of hypotension with remimazolam", "choice_b": "Lower odds of hypotension (OR 0.29; 95% CI 0.20\u20130.42) and bradycardia (OR 0.19) with remimazolam", "choice_c": "Significantly greater risk of postoperative delirium with remimazolam", "choice_d": "No differences in any secondary outcome", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["c", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "b"]}, "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1186/s13741-025-00589-7"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "A 46-year-old on chronic clonazepam for panic disorder missed his morning dose. Preoperatively he is tachycardic, anxious, and tremulous. The most appropriate CRNA action is to:", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Chronic benzodiazepine users can develop severe withdrawal (agitation, autonomic instability, delirium) with abrupt discontinuation (Yamaguchi et al., 2024). Continuation of baseline therapy or planned substitution is generally safer than unplanned discontinuation.", "source_ids": [13], "learning_objective_ids": [4, 5], "choice_a": "Cancel the case indefinitely", "choice_b": "Administer flumazenil to test dependence", "choice_c": "Continue baseline benzodiazepine therapy or provide equivalent substitution to prevent withdrawal", "choice_d": "Restrict all sedatives and proceed with general anesthesia", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "c"]}, "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1002/pcn5.70026"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Which statement best describes the 2023 Wang et al. BJA meta-analysis findings on perioperative benzodiazepines and postoperative delirium?", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The overall RR was 1.43 (95% CI 0.90\u20132.27), with very low certainty. This does not prove no harm. The dexmedetomidine comparison favored dexmedetomidine (RR 1.83; 95% CI 1.24\u20132.72 for benzodiazepines).", "source_ids": [8], "learning_objective_ids": [5], "choice_a": "Benzodiazepines significantly increased POD in all populations", "choice_b": "Benzodiazepines eliminated intraoperative awareness with no other effect", "choice_c": "Benzodiazepines were superior to propofol in preventing POD", "choice_d": "No statistically significant overall POD difference was found; risk was higher versus dexmedetomidine", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2023.04.017"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "An OSA patient with obesity and chronic opioid therapy is scheduled for endoscopy under MAC. Which sedation strategy is most appropriate?", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "In obesity + OSA + chronic opioid patients, benzodiazepines are risk-amplifying rather than benign. The safest approach minimizes or omits benzodiazepines and emphasizes airway preparation, capnography, opioid-sparing, and enhanced observation (Tracy et al., 2025).", "source_ids": [14], "learning_objective_ids": [4], "choice_a": "Minimize or avoid benzodiazepines, emphasize capnography, opioid-sparing plan, airway positioning, and enhanced monitoring", "choice_b": "Full-dose midazolam plus fentanyl bolus at start", "choice_c": "Administer flumazenil prophylactically after induction", "choice_d": "Use a fixed 5 mg midazolam premedication regardless of comorbidities", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["c", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "b"]}, "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1080/20565623.2025.2540744"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "In pregnancy, perioperative benzodiazepine use should generally follow which principle?", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Benzodiazepines cross the placenta. Bais et al. (2024) support a risk-benefit approach: use the lowest effective dose, avoid unnecessary repeat dosing, and coordinate care when clinically significant exposure is expected.", "source_ids": [15], "learning_objective_ids": [4], "choice_a": "Absolute contraindication throughout all trimesters", "choice_b": "Lowest effective dose, avoid unnecessary repeated dosing, and coordinate with obstetric/neonatal teams for significant exposure", "choice_c": "Routine high-dose use to reduce maternal stress", "choice_d": "Prefer diazepam because of its long half-life", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["c", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "b"]}, "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1007/s00404-024-07515-0"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "A postoperative lactating patient asks when she can resume breastfeeding after a single 2 mg midazolam preoperative dose. The best answer based on 2024 ASA guidance is:", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The 2024 ASA Statement on Resuming Breastfeeding After Anesthesia indicates most anesthetic and sedative medications do not require routine 'pump and dump.' After short-term perioperative benzodiazepine exposure, breastfeeding can resume once the patient is awake, alert, and able to hold the infant safely.", "source_ids": [16], "learning_objective_ids": [4], "choice_a": "Discard breast milk for 24 hours before resuming", "choice_b": "Discontinue breastfeeding permanently", "choice_c": "Resume breastfeeding once she is awake, alert, and able to safely hold the infant", "choice_d": "Pump and dump for 72 hours regardless of dose", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "c"]}, "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/standards-and-practice-parameters/statement-on-resuming-breastfeeding-after-anesthesia"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Which statement best describes remimazolam in a patient with severe hepatic impairment?", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Remimazolam is metabolized predominantly through carboxylesterases, including hepatic CES1. Severe hepatic impairment can prolong recovery; titrate carefully and consider less frequent supplemental doses.", "source_ids": [3, 4], "learning_objective_ids": [2, 4], "choice_a": "Its clearance cannot be affected because all esterases are outside the liver", "choice_b": "Normal adult doses guarantee normal recovery time", "choice_c": "Renal excretion of unchanged drug is the main determinant of clearance", "choice_d": "Carboxylesterase metabolism avoids major CYP involvement, but severe hepatic impairment can slow clearance", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "source_urls": ["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/212295s002lbl.pdf", "https://doi.org/10.1007/s13318-025-00963-2"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "In Zhang et al. (2025), the five-RCT emergence-delirium analysis compared remimazolam with placebo/saline or sevoflurane alone. RR 0.39 corresponds to approximately what relative reduction?", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "RR 0.39 corresponds to a 61% relative reduction (95% CI for RR 0.21\u20130.70). This emergence-delirium analysis did not use propofol as its comparator and does not establish a U.S. pediatric indication.", "source_ids": [11], "learning_objective_ids": [4, 5], "choice_a": "61%", "choice_b": "10%", "choice_c": "25%", "choice_d": "45%", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["c", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "b"]}, "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.3389/fped.2025.1662752"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Which of the following situations warrants the greatest caution before giving flumazenil?", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Dependence and mixed overdose increase withdrawal or seizure concerns. Flumazenil is contraindicated when there are signs of serious cyclic-antidepressant overdose or when a benzodiazepine is controlling a life-threatening condition such as status epilepticus.", "source_ids": [5], "learning_objective_ids": [5], "choice_a": "Isolated benzodiazepine sedation in a healthy adult", "choice_b": "Chronic benzodiazepine user or suspected mixed overdose with tricyclic exposure or seizure risk", "choice_c": "Delayed emergence after a single midazolam premedication dose", "choice_d": "Adult receiving flumazenil for procedural sedation reversal", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["c", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "b"]}, "source_urls": ["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=3ea41cbe-bc07-4c95-8bb2-7dfc2e96a874"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "The 2026 Zhou et al. review of remimazolam-flumazenil reversal reported that re-sedation may occur in approximately:", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The narrative review summarizes heterogeneous re-sedation rates around 2%\u201322%. Settings, definitions, doses, and exposure duration differ. Continue monitoring; initial arousal does not guarantee sustained recovery.", "source_ids": [12], "learning_objective_ids": [5], "choice_a": "<1% of cases", "choice_b": "50\u201380% of cases universally", "choice_c": "Approximately 2%\u201322% across heterogeneous study settings, not a universal patient risk", "choice_d": "Only after propofol co-administration", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "c"]}, "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2026.1793528"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "A 6-year-old child requires IV midazolam for procedural sedation. The typical initial IV dose is:", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "For children 6\u201312 years, initial IV midazolam is 0.025\u20130.05 mg/kg IV, cumulative up to about 0.4 mg/kg (usual max ~10 mg), titrated with close airway and ventilation monitoring, especially if opioids are coadministered.", "source_ids": [2], "learning_objective_ids": [3, 4], "choice_a": "0.005 mg/kg IV bolus", "choice_b": "0.2 mg/kg IV push", "choice_c": "1 mg/kg IV bolus", "choice_d": "0.025\u20130.05 mg/kg IV initially, titrated to effect", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "source_urls": ["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=94e1342b-f597-44c0-b8fc-b753fde04cbe"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Which statement about long-term versus preoperative benzodiazepine exposure in older adults is most consistent with the 2025 Starks et al. BioCog analysis?", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "In 928 BioCog participants, long-term use was associated with POD (p < 0.001), while immediate preoperative premedication was not significantly associated (p = 0.242). This observational analysis does not establish causation or prove universal safety.", "source_ids": [9], "learning_objective_ids": [5], "choice_a": "Long-term use was associated with POD, but immediate preoperative premedication was not significantly associated", "choice_b": "Both long-term use and single preoperative doses similarly increase POD", "choice_c": "Only preoperative premedication drives POD; long-term use is protective", "choice_d": "Neither long-term nor preoperative use has any effect on POD", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["c", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "b"]}, "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.1097/EJA.0000000000002201"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "An 82-year-old anxious patient with mild cognitive impairment is scheduled for hip fracture repair. The surgical team requests IV midazolam premedication. The most appropriate CRNA response is to:", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The 2025 APSF Brain Health advisory supports individualized decisions. A reduced titrated dose or nonpharmacologic strategies is often the best balance in a frail older adult with baseline cognitive impairment.", "source_ids": [2, 7], "learning_objective_ids": [4, 5], "choice_a": "Refuse all benzodiazepines and proceed with induction", "choice_b": "Consider a reduced dose (0.5\u20131 mg IV) or nonpharmacologic anxiolysis, individualized to frailty and cognition", "choice_c": "Give a 5 mg midazolam bolus to eliminate anxiety completely", "choice_d": "Administer flumazenil prophylactically before induction", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["c", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "b"]}, "source_urls": ["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=94e1342b-f597-44c0-b8fc-b753fde04cbe", "https://www.apsf.org/wp-content/uploads/newsletters/2025/4003/APSF4003-a07-Perioperative-Brain-Health.pdf"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which statement about remimazolam pharmacokinetics is correct?", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Remimazolam has a terminal half-life of approximately 37\u201353 minutes and clearance significantly higher than midazolam. It is CES1-metabolized and can be antagonized by flumazenil (Murrell & Harirforoosh, 2025).", "source_ids": [3, 4], "learning_objective_ids": [2], "choice_a": "It is metabolized primarily via renal excretion", "choice_b": "Its clearance is markedly lower than midazolam", "choice_c": "Terminal half-life is approximately 37\u201353 minutes", "choice_d": "It cannot be antagonized by flumazenil", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "c"]}, "source_urls": ["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2021/212295s002lbl.pdf", "https://doi.org/10.1007/s13318-025-00963-2"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "A child becomes sedated again after initially successful flumazenil reversal. Which statement is most appropriate?", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The label distinguishes initial pediatric titration from later repeat administration for re-sedation. The latter is not established. Continue observation and ventilatory support as needed, with individualized expert management.", "source_ids": [5], "learning_objective_ids": [3, 5], "choice_a": "Automatically repeat the adult 20-minute regimen", "choice_b": "Discharge because initial reversal proves the benzodiazepine effect is gone", "choice_c": "Flumazenil will reverse any co-administered opioid", "choice_d": "Support and monitor the airway; safety and efficacy of repeat flumazenil treatment for pediatric re-sedation are not established", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "source_urls": ["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=3ea41cbe-bc07-4c95-8bb2-7dfc2e96a874"]}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_3", "question_id": "benzo_25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Which statement best summarizes the CRNA's overall approach to perioperative benzodiazepine management in 2025\u20132026?", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Current best practice \u2014 grounded in Wang 2023, Ge 2025, Starks 2025, and the 2025 APSF Brain Health advisory \u2014 is individualized use rather than blanket avoidance or reflexive dosing. The core CRNA skill is matching agent, dose, and monitoring to the specific patient and documenting the reasoning.", "source_ids": [2, 7], "learning_objective_ids": [4, 5], "choice_a": "Individualize agent selection, dose, and monitoring based on age, comorbidities, chronic use, and procedural context; document reasoning", "choice_b": "Apply a single fixed premedication dose to every adult regardless of comorbidity", "choice_c": "Universally avoid all benzodiazepines in all populations", "choice_d": "Reverse every benzodiazepine dose with flumazenil at end of case", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["c", "d"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "b"]}, "source_urls": ["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=94e1342b-f597-44c0-b8fc-b753fde04cbe", "https://www.apsf.org/wp-content/uploads/newsletters/2025/4003/APSF4003-a07-Perioperative-Brain-Health.pdf"]}]''',
);
