ate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2024.1416998", "https://doi.org/10.1093/bjs/znaf079", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2207419", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2028788", "https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13741-024-00418-3", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13018-025-05942-6"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_5", "question_id": "course_1_module_5_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Which patient population is at HIGHEST risk of TXA-associated postoperative seizure?", "choice_a": "Pediatric dental extraction patients", "choice_b": "Healthy postpartum patients receiving 1 g IV", "choice_c": "Young orthopedic patients on topical TXA", "choice_d": "Older cardiac surgery patients with renal impairment on high-dose TXA", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Older age, cardiac surgery (particularly open-chamber), and renal impairment amplify seizure risk when combined with higher cumulative TXA doses (La Via et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2024.1416998", "https://doi.org/10.1093/bjs/znaf079", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2207419", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2028788", "https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13741-024-00418-3", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13018-025-05942-6"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_5", "question_id": "course_1_module_5_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Which statement best reflects the cited TXA injection label's ocular precautions?", "choice_a": "Report visual changes and consider ocular monitoring with prolonged use", "choice_b": "TXA treats retinal vein occlusion", "choice_c": "Ocular complications are impossible after IV dosing", "choice_d": "Visual symptoms should be ignored", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The label warns about retinal events and visual disturbances; consult the exact product information.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/fda/fdaDrugXsl.cfm?setid=246a3c6c-a7fb-45c7-9509-49a48a5bdcc6"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_5", "question_id": "course_1_module_5_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which prehospital TXA plan is most consistent with the 2025 joint statement?", "choice_a": "Give TXA for every minor injury", "choice_b": "Treat eligible hemorrhagic shock promptly, no later than 3 hours after injury", "choice_c": "Give TXA instead of immediate hemorrhage control", "choice_d": "Wait until a 2\u20133-hour window begins", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Prehospital TXA is an adjunct to lifesaving measures, not a reason to delay them.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [4], "source_urls": ["https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/10903127.2025.2497056"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_5", "question_id": "course_1_module_5_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which of the following is NOT part of the APSF/ISMP recommended safeguard set for wrong-route TXA errors?", "choice_a": "Route separation of TXA from neuraxial anesthetics", "choice_b": "Tall-man lettering and color-coded warning labels", "choice_c": "Colocating TXA and bupivacaine on the same tray for convenience", "choice_d": "Independent read-back before neuraxial injection", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Colocating TXA with local anesthetics is precisely the workflow failure APSF/ISMP identify as the root cause of catastrophic errors. All other options are recommended safeguards (APSF/ISMP, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2024.1416998", "https://doi.org/10.1093/bjs/znaf079", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2207419", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2028788", "https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13741-024-00418-3", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13018-025-05942-6"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_5", "question_id": "course_1_module_5_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "A 71-year-old cardiac surgery patient develops focal-onset seizures 4 hours postoperatively after TXA administration. The MOST appropriate initial management is:", "choice_a": "Immediate craniotomy", "choice_b": "Administer additional TXA to counteract the effect", "choice_c": "Discharge to the PACU with observation", "choice_d": "Airway support, benzodiazepine or propofol for seizure control, neurology consultation, and review of TXA dose and renal function", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Postoperative TXA-associated seizures require prompt airway management, benzodiazepine or propofol therapy, neurology consultation, and review of cumulative dose and renal function. Additional TXA is contraindicated (La Via et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2024.1416998", "https://doi.org/10.1093/bjs/znaf079", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2207419", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2028788", "https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13741-024-00418-3", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13018-025-05942-6"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_5", "question_id": "course_1_module_5_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Which single principle best summarizes the CRNA's role with TXA in modern practice?", "choice_a": "Match the right dose to the right patient by the right route at the right time, with system-level safeguards against wrong-route error", "choice_b": "Reserve TXA only for cardiac surgery", "choice_c": "Give the largest dose available whenever bleeding is possible", "choice_d": "Avoid TXA whenever possible because of seizure risk", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "TXA improves outcomes when the right dose reaches the right patient by the right route at the right time. This includes protecting against catastrophic wrong-route error through medication-system design (APSF/ISMP, 2025; Osawa et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2024.1416998", "https://doi.org/10.1093/bjs/znaf079", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2207419", "https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2028788", "https://doi.org/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13741-024-00418-3", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13018-025-05942-6"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}], "course_1_module_6": [{"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which of the following BEST distinguishes iodinated contrast media from gadolinium-based contrast agents (GBCAs) in perioperative practice?", "choice_a": "Iodinated agents are used for fluoroscopy/angiography; GBCAs are primarily used with MRI and carry NSF risk in severe kidney dysfunction", "choice_b": "GBCAs are used for CT; iodinated contrast is exclusive to ultrasound", "choice_c": "GBCAs and iodinated contrast are interchangeable in the OR", "choice_d": "Both are radiographic contrast; both share identical renal risk profiles", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Iodinated contrast is the standard for fluoroscopy and angiography, while GBCAs are used with MRI. GBCAs carry unique NSF risk in severe renal dysfunction, especially with older Group I agents (Welker et al., 2025; ACR Committee on Drugs and Contrast Media, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "Indocyanine green (ICG) is BEST described as:", "choice_a": "A local anesthetic dye for spinal use", "choice_b": "An optical/fluorescent imaging agent used with near-infrared systems for perfusion and biliary visualization", "choice_c": "A radiographic contrast agent used for angiography", "choice_d": "A gadolinium-based MR contrast agent", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "ICG is an optical imaging agent used with near-infrared fluorescence systems for perfusion assessment and biliary visualization (De Simone et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "A patient with a reported 'shellfish allergy' is scheduled for angiography. Which statement is MOST accurate?", "choice_a": "The procedure must be canceled", "choice_b": "The patient requires prophylactic dialysis before contrast", "choice_c": "Shellfish allergy does not, by itself, establish a true contrast allergy; prior reaction to the same class of contrast is the most useful history", "choice_d": "Shellfish allergy is a definite contraindication to iodinated contrast", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "AAAAI and current allergy literature confirm that shellfish or generic 'iodine' allergy does not establish contrast allergy. The clinically useful history is prior reaction to the same class of contrast agent (AAAAI, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "Which category BEST describes an ACR-defined SEVERE acute contrast reaction?", "choice_a": "Diffuse urticaria with mild bronchospasm", "choice_b": "Skin discoloration only", "choice_c": "Limited urticaria and mild nausea", "choice_d": "Laryngeal edema with stridor/hypoxia, severe bronchospasm with hypoxia, or anaphylactic shock", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Severity depends on physiologic compromise; throat symptoms without dyspnea are not equivalent to laryngeal edema with stridor or hypoxia.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://geiselmed.dartmouth.edu/radiology/wp-content/uploads/sites/47/2024/08/ACR-contrast-2024.pdf"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Preferred terminology for kidney injury temporally associated with contrast administration is:", "choice_a": "Contrast-associated acute kidney injury (CA-AKI) to reflect temporal association without assuming direct causation", "choice_b": "Post-renal failure", "choice_c": "Acute tubular necrosis", "choice_d": "Contrast-induced acute kidney injury (CI-AKI) in all cases", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Contemporary sources prefer CA-AKI to acknowledge that perioperative AKI often has multiple competing causes \u2014 hypotension, sepsis, nephrotoxins \u2014 beyond the contrast itself (ACR Committee on Drugs and Contrast Media, 2025; Frontiers in Nephrology, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Which patient scenario carries the HIGHEST risk of CA-AKI after iodinated contrast?", "choice_a": "eGFR 90, elective outpatient CT", "choice_b": "eGFR 22, recent AKI, and intraoperative hypotension", "choice_c": "eGFR 65, euvolemic, stable hemodynamics", "choice_d": "eGFR 55, stable, oral hydration", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Advanced CKD (eGFR <30), recent AKI, and major hemodynamic instability represent the highest-risk stratum for CA-AKI (ESUR Contrast Media Safety Committee, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "Under ACR guidance, a patient taking metformin has no AKI and stable eGFR 55 before IV contrast CT. What is appropriate?", "choice_a": "Substitute insulin for the day", "choice_b": "Withhold metformin for 48 hours before and after contrast", "choice_c": "Continue metformin; no routine hold is required at this eGFR with IV contrast", "choice_d": "Cancel the study", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The 2024 ACR framework and the 2025 Xu et al. systematic review support continuing metformin in patients with eGFR \u226530 and stable renal function receiving IV or below-the-diaphragm intra-arterial contrast (ACR Committee on Drugs and Contrast Media, 2025; Xu et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://geiselmed.dartmouth.edu/radiology/wp-content/uploads/sites/47/2024/08/ACR-contrast-2024.pdf"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Under the ACR framework, how should metformin be managed with iodinated contrast in a patient with eGFR 24?", "choice_a": "Restart automatically at 48 hours even if eGFR remains below 30", "choice_b": "Hold only until the contrast injection ends", "choice_c": "Continue without reassessment", "choice_d": "Withhold at/before contrast and for 48 hours; reassess renal function and eligibility", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "AKI or eGFR below 30 requires reassessment; a 48-hour interval does not itself make restarting safe.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://geiselmed.dartmouth.edu/radiology/wp-content/uploads/sites/47/2024/08/ACR-contrast-2024.pdf"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "The primary unique concern with gadolinium-based contrast agents in patients with severe kidney dysfunction is:", "choice_a": "Nephrogenic systemic fibrosis (NSF), particularly with older Group I agents", "choice_b": "Contrast-induced hepatic injury", "choice_c": "Immediate anaphylaxis risk higher than iodinated contrast", "choice_d": "Cardiac arrhythmias", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The dominant CRNA concern with GBCAs in advanced CKD is NSF, highest with older, less-stable Group I agents. Group II macrocyclic GBCAs carry substantially lower risk (Welker et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "According to the 2025 ASNR position statement, GBCA use in stable CKD patients who require MRI:", "choice_a": "Is absolutely contraindicated regardless of agent", "choice_b": "Use a clinically appropriate Group II agent; the group includes macrocyclic and some linear agents", "choice_c": "Is safe only in dialysis-dependent patients", "choice_d": "Requires mandatory hemodialysis after every dose", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "NSF-risk groups are not synonymous with chemical structure. Do not start prophylactic dialysis solely for Group II contrast.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://geiselmed.dartmouth.edu/radiology/wp-content/uploads/sites/47/2024/08/ACR-contrast-2024.pdf"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Methylene blue can precipitate serotonin toxicity primarily by:", "choice_a": "Direct 5-HT release from platelets", "choice_b": "Blocking dopamine reuptake", "choice_c": "Inhibiting monoamine oxidase (MAO)", "choice_d": "Antagonizing serotonin receptors", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Methylene blue is a potent MAO inhibitor and can precipitate serotonin toxicity when combined with serotonergic agents (APSF, 2024; Francescangeli et al., 2016).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Which medication combination with methylene blue carries the HIGHEST risk of serotonin toxicity?", "choice_a": "Beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker", "choice_b": "Aspirin and clopidogrel", "choice_c": "Acetaminophen and ondansetron", "choice_d": "SSRI, SNRI, MAOI, linezolid, or certain TCAs", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Review serotonergic medications and opioids; the PROVAYBLUE boxed warning advises avoiding concomitant use because serious serotonin syndrome can occur.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [2], "source_urls": ["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/204630Orig1s023correctedlbl.pdf"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "A patient on chronic SSRI receives intraoperative methylene blue and develops hypertension, agitation, clonus, and hyperthermia in PACU. The MOST likely diagnosis is:", "choice_a": "Serotonin toxicity related to methylene blue and SSRI co-exposure", "choice_b": "Postoperative delirium unrelated to medications", "choice_c": "Malignant hyperthermia", "choice_d": "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The clinical triad of autonomic instability, neuromuscular changes (clonus, rigidity), and altered mental status after methylene blue in an SSRI-treated patient strongly suggests serotonin toxicity (Francescangeli et al., 2016).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "What is the appropriate response when PROVAYBLUE is planned for a patient with known G6PD deficiency?", "choice_a": "Use it to correct G6PD deficiency", "choice_b": "Identify the contraindication, notify the team, and select an alternative", "choice_c": "Give half the usual dose without discussion", "choice_d": "Proceed because dye doses cannot cause hemolysis", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "PROVAYBLUE is contraindicated because severe hemolysis can occur and treatment may be ineffective.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [2], "source_urls": ["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2024/204630Orig1s023correctedlbl.pdf"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "Indocyanine green anaphylaxis during intraoperative use is BEST described as:", "choice_a": "Only reported in ophthalmic procedures", "choice_b": "The most common cause of intraoperative reactions", "choice_c": "Rare but documented; ICG should be included in the intraoperative hypersensitivity differential", "choice_d": "Impossible \u2014 ICG has no allergenic potential", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "ICG anaphylaxis is rare but well-documented in abdominal, colorectal, and other perioperative settings. Rare does not mean irrelevant \u2014 include ICG in the intraoperative reaction differential (Anaphylaxis Induced by ICG PMC report, 2024; De Simone et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "A patient receiving ICG during robotic colorectal surgery develops sudden erythema, increasing airway pressures, and hypotension. The MOST appropriate response is:", "choice_a": "Reverse anesthesia immediately", "choice_b": "Administer additional ICG to confirm the reaction", "choice_c": "Continue surgery \u2014 ICG cannot cause anaphylaxis", "choice_d": "Stop exposure, call for help, support airway/circulation, and give epinephrine using the perioperative anaphylaxis protocol", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "This pattern requires urgent anaphylaxis treatment. Epinephrine route and dosing depend on the setting; titrated IV dosing is for trained clinicians with appropriate monitoring, not interchangeable with IM dosing.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Fluorescein administration is MOST likely to cause:", "choice_a": "Nausea, vomiting, and skin/urine discoloration; occasional severe hypersensitivity", "choice_b": "Immediate anaphylaxis in every patient", "choice_c": "Renal failure in all patients", "choice_d": "Cardiac arrest as the most common reaction", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The most common fluorescein reactions are nausea, vomiting, and skin/urine discoloration. Severe hypersensitivity is uncommon but documented (Ge & O'Shea, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Which of the following is the MOST important historical risk factor for recurrent iodinated contrast reaction?", "choice_a": "Shellfish allergy", "choice_b": "Prior reaction to the same class of iodinated contrast agent", "choice_c": "Asthma alone", "choice_d": "Multiple unrelated drug allergies", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "A prior reaction to the same class of contrast agent is the single most important predictor of recurrence \u2014 more predictive than asthma, unrelated allergies, or shellfish history (ACR Committee on Drugs and Contrast Media, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "Contemporary guidance on premedication for prior contrast reactions:", "choice_a": "Eliminates the need for reaction preparedness", "choice_b": "Recommends universal premedication for all allergic patients", "choice_c": "Is more selective \u2014 targeted premedication for high-risk histories, with understanding that it does not eliminate reaction risk", "choice_d": "Requires 48 hours of steroid loading in every case", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Contemporary guidance is more selective, and premedication does not eliminate reaction risk. Readiness to treat an acute event remains essential regardless of premedication (ACR Committee on Drugs and Contrast Media, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "A 72-year-old undergoing endovascular aneurysm repair develops sudden hypotension, tachycardia, and bronchospasm immediately after iodinated contrast injection. The MOST appropriate initial action is:", "choice_a": "Deepen anesthesia and continue", "choice_b": "Assume hemorrhage and give massive transfusion first", "choice_c": "Continue contrast to complete imaging", "choice_d": "Stop exposure, call for help, support airway/circulation, and treat with epinephrine per the perioperative anaphylaxis protocol", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Treat anaphylaxis promptly while assessing other causes. Route, dose, and monitoring must follow the perioperative protocol; IM and IV doses are not interchangeable.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Extravasation of iodinated contrast at the injection site should be managed with:", "choice_a": "Stopping injection, elevating the extremity, assessing for compartment syndrome or severe tissue injury, and documenting", "choice_b": "Continued injection to flush the extravasated volume", "choice_c": "No specific action \u2014 extravasation is always benign", "choice_d": "Immediate surgical debridement in every case", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Extravasation management includes stopping injection, elevation, assessment for compartment syndrome or severe injury, and clear documentation. Most events are self-limited but require attention (ACR Committee on Drugs and Contrast Media, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "For a patient scheduled for MRI with GBCA who has stable eGFR 40 mL/min/1.73m\u00b2, the BEST approach according to the 2025 ASNR statement is:", "choice_a": "Substitute iodinated contrast for MRI", "choice_b": "Use an appropriate Group II GBCA when indicated; routine prophylactic dialysis is not required", "choice_c": "Cancel the MRI entirely", "choice_d": "Require pre-procedure dialysis for every patient", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Group II agents have very low NSF risk and include macrocyclic and some linear agents. Individualize selection with radiology.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://geiselmed.dartmouth.edu/radiology/wp-content/uploads/sites/47/2024/08/ACR-contrast-2024.pdf"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which of the following BEST captures the CRNA preoperative approach when contrast or dye use is anticipated?", "choice_a": "Give universal steroid premedication regardless of history", "choice_b": "Delay all cases involving contrast", "choice_c": "Verify agent, indication, route/dose, and screen for prior reaction, advanced kidney disease, and high-risk medications (serotonergic co-therapy if methylene blue possible)", "choice_d": "No pre-op planning is required \u2014 contrast is a radiology-side issue", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Structured pre-case verification \u2014 agent, indication, route, and focused history \u2014 is the CRNA's core preparation for any perioperative contrast or dye use.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Which observation plan is appropriate during contrast or ICG administration?", "choice_a": "Observe only if premedication was omitted", "choice_b": "Wait for skin findings before assessing circulation", "choice_c": "Stop monitoring after 5 minutes", "choice_d": "Maintain immediate and continued vigilance; reactions are not limited to 5\u201310 minutes", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Monitor the patient throughout the clinically appropriate period and respond promptly to airway or cardiovascular changes.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://geiselmed.dartmouth.edu/radiology/wp-content/uploads/sites/47/2024/08/ACR-contrast-2024.pdf"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_6", "question_id": "course_1_module_6_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Which single principle BEST summarizes the CRNA's role with perioperative contrast and operative dyes?", "choice_a": "Treat every intravascular contrast or dye exposure as part of the anesthetic environment \u2014 anticipating reactions, individualizing renal-risk assessment, and screening for high-risk drug interactions", "choice_b": "Treat contrast as a radiology-side detail unrelated to anesthesia", "choice_c": "Focus only on hemodynamics; ignore renal and drug-interaction considerations", "choice_d": "Avoid all contrast use whenever possible", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Every intravascular contrast or dye is part of the anesthetic environment. The CRNA anticipates reactions, individualizes renal risk, screens for drug interactions (especially methylene blue with serotonergic agents), and prepares for immediate management of hypersensitivity (ACR Committee on Drugs and Contrast Media, 2025; APSF, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.aaaai.org/tools-for-the-public/conditions-library/allergies/radiocontrast", "https://www.acr.org/Clinical-Resources/Clinical-Tools-and-Reference/Contrast-Manual", "https://www.apsf.org/article/methylene-blue-and-the-risk-of-serotonin-toxicity/", "https://doi.org/10.1186/s13017-025-00583-w", "https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/011525Orig1s039lbl.pdf", "https://www.esur.org/esur-guidelines-2025/", "https://doi.org/10.12659/AJCR.897298", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fneph.2025.1582775", "https://doi.org/10.3390/molecules29245964", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12017462/", "https://doi.org/10.3174/ajnr.A8578", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2025.1547725", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12568759/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11587973/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}], "course_1_module_7": [{"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which cannabis history is most useful before anesthesia?", "choice_a": "Product, route, amount, frequency, and exact time of last use", "choice_b": "Only whether use is legal in the patient's state", "choice_c": "Only whether the patient calls it medical cannabis", "choice_d": "No history if a urine screen is negative", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Universal, specific screening is more useful than legal-status counts or assumptions about a product.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://rapm.bmj.com/content/early/2023/01/03/rapm-2022-104013"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "Which cannabinoid is the principal psychoactive compound and drives most of the acute intoxication, cognitive impairment, and tachycardia associated with cannabis?", "choice_a": "Cannabinol (CBN)", "choice_b": "Tetrahydrocannabinol (THC)", "choice_c": "Cannabigerol (CBG)", "choice_d": "Cannabidiol (CBD)", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "THC is the principal psychoactive compound and is primarily responsible for intoxication, altered perception, impaired cognition, and tachycardia (Echeverria-Villalobos et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "CBD is often perceived as therapeutically benign, but which anesthesia-relevant pharmacologic property makes it clinically important?", "choice_a": "CBD is an irreversible acetylcholinesterase inhibitor", "choice_b": "CBD is a potent MAO inhibitor", "choice_c": "CBD is pharmacologically active and may alter co-medication exposure through enzyme-mediated interactions", "choice_d": "CBD has no pharmacologic activity at any human receptor", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Interaction magnitude depends on product, dose, and the co-medication. Review the exact drug rather than assuming all predicted interactions are equally established.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://rapm.bmj.com/content/early/2023/01/03/rapm-2022-104013"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "CB1 receptors are BEST described as:", "choice_a": "Peripheral receptors concentrated only in the liver", "choice_b": "Immune-only receptors with no CNS relevance", "choice_c": "Enzymes rather than receptors", "choice_d": "Central nervous system receptors that modulate memory, mood, pain processing, appetite, and autonomic tone", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "CB1 receptors are concentrated in the CNS and modulate memory, mood, pain processing, appetite, and autonomic tone. Their activation is central to the anesthesia-relevant effects of THC (Cannabinoids and General Anesthetics review, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Which route of cannabis administration is MOST associated with delayed, unpredictable absorption and a longer duration of intoxication?", "choice_a": "Oral edibles", "choice_b": "Sublingual tincture", "choice_c": "Smoked flower", "choice_d": "Vaporized concentrate", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Oral edibles have onset 30\u2013120 minutes and duration 4\u20138 hours or more, with more unpredictable absorption than inhaled or sublingual routes (Echeverria-Villalobos et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "A patient smoked cannabis 60 minutes before an elective outpatient procedure and now has HR 118, mild hypertension, and delayed responses. According to ASRA 2023 guidance, the MOST appropriate action is:", "choice_a": "Proceed as planned; there is no evidence-based reason to delay", "choice_b": "Postpone the elective surgery until the patient is clinically sober and able to participate in informed consent", "choice_c": "Give a benzodiazepine and proceed", "choice_d": "Cancel all future surgeries indefinitely", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "ASRA 2023 recommends postponing elective surgery in patients with altered mental status, impaired capacity, or acute intoxication related to cannabis. A 2-hour minimum after smoked cannabis is a floor, not a ceiling (Shah et al., 2023).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "What is the guideline-consistent MINIMUM delay recommended after smoked cannabis before proceeding with elective surgery?", "choice_a": "7 days", "choice_b": "24 hours", "choice_c": "2 hours", "choice_d": "30 minutes", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "ASRA-aligned guidance advises a minimum 2-hour delay after smoked cannabis before elective surgery because acute smoking increases heart rate, blood pressure, and airway irritation during a high-risk anesthesia window (Shah et al., 2023).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Which of the following is the MOST accurate summary of postoperative pain expectations in chronic heavy cannabis users?", "choice_a": "Pain outcomes are identical regardless of cannabis use", "choice_b": "Cannabis eliminates postoperative pain entirely", "choice_c": "Chronic cannabis users reliably require less opioid than non-users", "choice_d": "Chronic cannabis users may have HIGHER postoperative pain scores and greater opioid requirements, so multimodal analgesia should be planned proactively", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Contemporary reviews and observational data show chronic cannabis users may report higher postoperative pain scores and require MORE opioid \u2014 not less. Multimodal analgesia should be planned upfront (Preoperative Cannabinoid Exposure narrative review, 2026; Association of Cannabis Use After Discharge, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "Which physiologic pattern is MOST characteristic of acute smoked cannabis exposure and relevant to induction planning?", "choice_a": "Tachycardia, hypertension, and airway irritation", "choice_b": "Bradycardia and hypotension", "choice_c": "Hypothermia and diaphoresis", "choice_d": "Hyperreflexia and clonus", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Acute smoked cannabis characteristically produces tachycardia, hypertension, and airway irritation, which can complicate induction and increase perioperative cardiovascular risk (Alkhouli et al., 2025; APSF, 2026).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "How should propofol dosing be approached in a habitual cannabis user?", "choice_a": "Automatically double the induction dose", "choice_b": "Titrate to effect; heterogeneous studies suggest higher average use but no fixed increment", "choice_c": "Assume chronic use eliminates respiratory depression", "choice_d": "Use the same fixed increase for every route and product", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "The 2025 review combined sedation and general-anesthesia data and does not establish a universal induction-dose rule.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [2], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Which airway concern is MOST relevant in chronic cannabis smokers or vapers during airway instrumentation?", "choice_a": "Guaranteed absence of airway reactivity", "choice_b": "Fixed upper-airway obstruction unrelated to inhaled products", "choice_c": "Airway hyperreactivity, bronchospasm, and increased secretions", "choice_d": "Complete loss of laryngeal reflexes", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Inhaled cannabis can cause chronic cough, wheeze, sputum production, and airway hyperreactivity. Bronchospasm and increased secretions are reasonable perioperative concerns (APSF, 2026; Echeverria-Villalobos et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "A patient reports classic cyclic vomiting relieved only by prolonged hot showers in the setting of daily cannabis use for five years. This history MOST strongly suggests:", "choice_a": "Routine postoperative nausea and vomiting", "choice_b": "Serotonin syndrome", "choice_c": "Acute appendicitis", "choice_d": "Cannabis hyperemesis syndrome (CHS)", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "The pattern supports CHS, but hot-water relief alone is not diagnostic. Assess alternative causes, hydration, and electrolytes.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [3], "source_urls": ["https://www.acep.org/toxicology/newsroom/april-2024/cannabinoid-hyperemesis-syndrome"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "Which antiemetic strategy is MOST appropriate when a patient with cannabis hyperemesis syndrome does not respond to ondansetron?", "choice_a": "Consider alternative agents such as haloperidol or topical capsaicin, along with fluid and electrolyte support", "choice_b": "Add another 5-HT3 antagonist and hope for the best", "choice_c": "Administer a large opioid dose", "choice_d": "Withhold all antiemetics", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Refractory CHS may require alternatives plus fluid/electrolyte treatment. Assess QT and other adverse-effect risks for the selected agent. Cannabis cessation is central to definitive management.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [3], "source_urls": ["https://www.acep.org/toxicology/newsroom/april-2024/cannabinoid-hyperemesis-syndrome"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "An adult presents intoxicated and confused before an elective procedure. Regarding informed consent, the MOST accurate statement is:", "choice_a": "Family members can always consent on behalf of an alert but intoxicated adult", "choice_b": "Acute cannabis intoxication may impair decision-making capacity, so elective surgery should be postponed until the patient can meaningfully participate in consent", "choice_c": "Documentation of intoxication is unnecessary", "choice_d": "Acute intoxication has no bearing on consent", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Acute cannabis intoxication may impair attention, judgment, and the ability to understand information \u2014 undermining valid informed consent. Elective procedures should be postponed until the patient is clinically sober (Shah et al., 2023).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "A daily heavy cannabis user abruptly stops using 24 hours before surgery. Which withdrawal features should the CRNA anticipate perioperatively?", "choice_a": "Guaranteed seizures", "choice_b": "Complete asymptomatic withdrawal in all patients", "choice_c": "Irritability, anxiety, sleep disturbance, nausea, and restlessness", "choice_d": "Fixed dilated pupils and hyperthermia", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Abrupt cannabis cessation in heavy users can cause irritability, anxiety, sleep disturbance, nausea, and restlessness beginning 1\u20132 days after cessation and lasting up to 2 weeks (Cleveland Clinic, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Which of the following is MOST consistent with the ASRA 2023 approach to cannabis screening in the preoperative interview?", "choice_a": "Skip cannabis entirely if the patient does not volunteer it", "choice_b": "Rely on routine urine toxicology in all patients", "choice_c": "Ask only patients who 'look like they use cannabis'", "choice_d": "Universal specific screening of every patient \u2014 product, route, frequency, and time of last use", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "ASRA 2023 recommends universal, specific screening \u2014 not selective questioning based on appearance. Routine toxicology is not recommended for all patients, but evaluation for acute intoxication is essential (Shah et al., 2023).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "A patient is scheduled for an urgent hip fixation and smoked cannabis 3 hours ago. The MOST appropriate action is to:", "choice_a": "Treat the patient according to current physiologic risk rather than delaying for cannabis exposure alone", "choice_b": "Refuse to provide anesthesia care", "choice_c": "Demand a several-week cannabis-free period before any care", "choice_d": "Cancel the case indefinitely", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "For urgent or emergent cases, the algorithm supports proceeding based on current physiologic risk rather than delaying solely because of recent cannabis exposure. Document capacity considerations if intoxication is present (Shah et al., 2023).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Which of the following BEST describes contemporary evidence on cannabis and cardiovascular risk relevant to perioperative planning?", "choice_a": "Only smoked forms have any physiologic effects at all", "choice_b": "Cannabis use is associated with elevated risk of myocardial infarction and adverse cardiovascular events, particularly in younger adults and heavier users", "choice_c": "Cannabis is now considered cardioprotective at any dose", "choice_d": "Cannabis has no cardiovascular effects", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Large-scale 2025 analyses continue to link cannabis with elevated MI and adverse cardiovascular event risk, especially in younger adults and heavier users (Alkhouli et al., 2025; ACC, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "Regarding preoperative counseling of a patient using medical cannabis for chronic pain, the MOST appropriate approach is to:", "choice_a": "Ignore the medical context entirely", "choice_b": "Apply a rigid multi-week stop rule to every medical cannabis user", "choice_c": "Individualize the plan by clarifying indication, product, and consequences of abrupt cessation such as worsened pain, nausea, anxiety, or sleep", "choice_d": "Refuse to care for any medical cannabis patient", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Medical cannabis users should be counseled individually. The goal is safer timing, honest disclosure, and individualized planning \u2014 not a punitive universal stop rule (Shah et al., 2023).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Which combination is MOST important to warn patients against in the immediate postoperative recovery period?", "choice_a": "Cannabis combined with plain water", "choice_b": "Cannabis combined with acetaminophen", "choice_c": "Cannabis combined with topical antibiotics", "choice_d": "Cannabis combined with prescribed opioids, benzodiazepines, sleep aids, muscle relaxants, or alcohol", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Patients should be advised not to combine cannabis with opioids, benzodiazepines, sleep aids, muscle relaxants, or alcohol during recovery \u2014 the combination increases drowsiness, dizziness, falls, and impaired recognition of complications (APSF, 2026).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Which recovery-period symptom pattern in a chronic cannabis user should MOST prompt evaluation for cannabis withdrawal rather than routine emergence issues?", "choice_a": "Irritability, anxiety, sleep disturbance, and nausea emerging 24\u201348 hours after abrupt cessation", "choice_b": "Isolated shoulder pain unrelated to procedure", "choice_c": "Rapid resolution of all symptoms without intervention", "choice_d": "Isolated postoperative pruritus", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Cannabis withdrawal typically emerges within 1\u20132 days of cessation and features irritability, anxiety, sleep disturbance, nausea, and restlessness (Cleveland Clinic, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which of the following BEST reflects contemporary guidance on universal preoperative cannabis abstinence duration?", "choice_a": "Cannabis must be held for at least 6 months before any elective procedure", "choice_b": "Current evidence does not support a single universal long-term hold; the focus is on avoiding surgery during acute intoxication and immediate physiologic effects", "choice_c": "A universal 30-day cessation period is mandated for every patient", "choice_d": "No cessation is ever recommended", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Contemporary guidance does not support a universal long-term hold. The focus is on avoiding elective surgery during acute intoxication and immediate physiologic effects (Shah et al., 2023).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "A CBD-heavy medical cannabis user is scheduled for surgery on multiple medications including a direct oral anticoagulant and an antiepileptic. The MOST anesthesia-relevant drug-interaction concern is:", "choice_a": "CBD reliably decreases exposure of all co-administered drugs", "choice_b": "CBD has no pharmacokinetic interactions", "choice_c": "CBD-mediated CYP450 inhibition may alter exposure of co-administered drugs including some antiepileptics and anticoagulants", "choice_d": "CBD is only relevant for topical medications", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Review each interaction and evidence level. Do not abruptly stop prescribed cannabinoid antiepileptic therapy; coordinate the plan with the prescriber.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://rapm.bmj.com/content/early/2023/01/03/rapm-2022-104013"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Which single sentence BEST summarizes the CRNA's practical role with perioperative cannabis?", "choice_a": "Ignore cannabis exposure \u2014 it is not anesthesia-relevant", "choice_b": "Refuse to care for any cannabis user", "choice_c": "Apply a universal 30-day stop rule to every patient", "choice_d": "Screen universally with product-level detail, postpone elective surgery for acute intoxication, plan multimodal analgesia, and individualize care", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Universal specific screening, postponing elective surgery for intoxication, proactive multimodal analgesia, and individualized care form the ASRA-aligned CRNA approach to perioperative cannabis (Shah et al., 2023; APSF, 2026).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_7", "question_id": "course_1_module_7_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "A patient scheduled for elective ambulatory surgery admits to smoking cannabis 90 minutes before arrival, denies intoxication, but has HR 112 and mild hypertension. Which of the following BEST reflects the ASRA-consistent decision pathway?", "choice_a": "Delay at least until the 2-hour post-smoke floor has passed and physiologic signs have normalized; extend the delay if tachycardia or hypertension persist", "choice_b": "Sedate the patient heavily until symptoms disappear and proceed", "choice_c": "Cancel indefinitely and refuse future care", "choice_d": "Proceed immediately regardless of hemodynamics", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "A minimum 2-hour delay after smoked cannabis is guideline-consistent, but persistent tachycardia or hypertension warrants further delay. The 2-hour floor is not a ceiling (Shah et al., 2023; APSF, 2026).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.jacc.org/doi/10.1016/j.jacadv.2025.101698", "https://www.acc.org/latest-in-cardiology/journal-scans/2025/03/24/13/52/cannabis", "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/marijuana-weed-withdrawal", "https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF12270", "https://doi.org/10.3389/fphar.2024.1282831", "https://journals.lww.com/anesthesia-", "https://emcrit.org/ibcc/cannabis/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11867069/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11818839/", "https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41406677/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38771607/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36596580/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37638417/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12063680/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}], "course_1_module_8": [{"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which of the following BEST describes the modern illicit drug supply that CRNAs now encounter perioperatively?", "choice_a": "A mixed, unstable, and often adulterated environment dominated by illicit fentanyl and polysubstance exposure", "choice_b": "Almost exclusively heroin and cocaine", "choice_c": "Well-labeled prescription products obtained outside pharmacies", "choice_d": "A predictable set of pure single-drug exposures", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The modern era is dominated by illicit fentanyl, polysubstance combinations, and adulterants such as xylazine and nitazenes \u2014 reported drug names often underestimate actual exposure (CDC, 2024; Perrone et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "A patient reports \"just a little cocaine\" 45 minutes before scheduled elective surgery and has HR 130, BP 195/108, and chest pain. The MOST appropriate action is:", "choice_a": "Give a beta-blocker and proceed", "choice_b": "Postpone elective surgery and evaluate for stimulant-related cardiac ischemia", "choice_c": "Proceed immediately with induction", "choice_d": "Cancel all future surgery for this patient", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Acute stimulant intoxication with chest pain, tachycardia, and hypertension warrants postponement and workup for coronary vasospasm or ischemia; do not proceed with elective surgery (Mishra et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Chronic methamphetamine users may respond to induction UNEXPECTEDLY with:", "choice_a": "Immediate hyperkalemia", "choice_b": "Malignant hyperthermia only", "choice_c": "Hypotension due to catecholamine depletion", "choice_d": "Persistent extreme hypertension", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Chronic stimulant use can deplete catecholamine stores, so patients may be hypertensive on arrival but become unexpectedly hypotensive after induction. Titrate and be prepared to support blood pressure (WFSA ATOTW, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "Which best describes xylazine's clinical relevance to the perioperative CRNA?", "choice_a": "It is a fully naloxone-reversible opioid", "choice_b": "It is only relevant in pediatric patients", "choice_c": "It causes sympathomimetic toxicity indistinguishable from cocaine", "choice_d": "It is a veterinary alpha-2 agonist increasingly adulterating illicit fentanyl; naloxone does NOT reverse its effects", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Xylazine is a veterinary alpha-2 agonist adulterant increasingly in the illicit opioid supply. It contributes to sedation, bradycardia, hypotension, and respiratory depression, and naloxone reverses only the opioid component (Solanki et al., 2024; CDC, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "After naloxone for suspected fentanyl overdose, a patient remains sedated with RR 8. What is the best next step?", "choice_a": "Support ventilation and repeat/titrate naloxone as needed while assessing co-exposure and other causes", "choice_b": "Use full alertness as the only treatment endpoint", "choice_c": "Assume xylazine and stop all naloxone", "choice_d": "Wait without airway support", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Residual or recurrent opioid effect remains possible. Naloxone does not reverse xylazine, but suspected xylazine does not remove the need for opioid reversal.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/media/pdfs/2024/07/Xylazine-Clinical-Management-and-Harm-Reduction.pdf"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Nitazenes are BEST described as:", "choice_a": "Non-opioid analgesics with a wide safety margin", "choice_b": "Highly potent synthetic opioids increasingly in the illicit supply, with risk of profound tolerance and refractory overdose", "choice_c": "A class of veterinary alpha-2 agonists", "choice_d": "Benzodiazepines used in club settings", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Nitazenes are highly potent synthetic opioids increasingly identified in the illicit drug supply. They may produce profound tolerance, refractory overdose, and unpredictable sedation (Hamza et al., 2026).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "A patient with daily illicit fentanyl use undergoes ORIF and reports 10/10 pain in PACU despite standard opioid dosing. The MOST appropriate approach is:", "choice_a": "Discharge with home NSAIDs only", "choice_b": "Assume drug-seeking and withhold further analgesia", "choice_c": "Provide multimodal analgesia (regional if appropriate, acetaminophen, NSAIDs where safe, ketamine adjunct) and titrate opioids to effect", "choice_d": "Give flumazenil", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Chronic illicit fentanyl use produces marked tolerance and often opioid-induced hyperalgesia. Multimodal analgesia is essential, and opioid requirements are often higher than expected (Hooten, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Opioid withdrawal is BEST characterized as:", "choice_a": "Rarely symptomatic", "choice_b": "Indistinguishable from stimulant intoxication", "choice_c": "Reliably life-threatening in every patient", "choice_d": "Typically distressing but not directly life-threatening in most patients \u2014 still important to treat perioperatively", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Opioid withdrawal is usually not directly life-threatening but is severe, distressing, and disruptive perioperatively, and can complicate hemodynamics and pain control (Regina et al., 2024; Hooten, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "Which withdrawal syndrome carries the HIGHEST risk of life-threatening deterioration such as seizures and severe autonomic instability?", "choice_a": "Sedative-hypnotic and GHB withdrawal", "choice_b": "Cannabis withdrawal", "choice_c": "Opioid withdrawal in an otherwise healthy adult", "choice_d": "Stimulant crash", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Sedative-hypnotic and GHB withdrawal can progress to severe agitation, autonomic instability, hallucinations, delirium, and seizures \u2014 high-acuity monitoring and escalation are often required (Karila et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "A postoperative patient with chronic GHB use becomes rapidly agitated, tremulous, and autonomically unstable. The BEST interpretation is:", "choice_a": "Routine PACU emergence delirium", "choice_b": "Possible GHB withdrawal requiring urgent escalation of monitoring and treatment", "choice_c": "Malingering", "choice_d": "Simple postoperative anxiety", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "GHB withdrawal onset may be rapid and severe. In a chronic user showing escalating agitation and autonomic instability, escalate monitoring \u2014 do not assume emergence delirium (Karila et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "Which route of illicit drug use MOST directly heightens concern for infective endocarditis, bacteremia, and difficult IV access?", "choice_a": "Oral pill", "choice_b": "Sublingual", "choice_c": "Injection", "choice_d": "Intranasal", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Injection drug use is associated with infective endocarditis, bacteremia, skin and soft tissue infection, hepatitis, HIV, septic emboli, and poor venous access (Quintana et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Which of the following is the STRONGEST reason to suspect that a reported \"Xanax\" or \"Percocet\" pill purchased outside a pharmacy could contain fentanyl, methamphetamine, or nitazenes?", "choice_a": "Counterfeit pills only affect adolescents", "choice_b": "Only prescription bottles from other countries contain adulterants", "choice_c": "Reported pill names are always accurate", "choice_d": "Counterfeit pill supplies are known to be pharmacologically unreliable and commonly contain unexpected substances", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Counterfeit tablets frequently contain fentanyl, methamphetamine, nitazenes, or other unexpected substances. Treat counterfeit-pill exposure as pharmacologically uncertain (CDC, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "A patient with acute PCP intoxication presents to the OR. Which finding is MOST expected?", "choice_a": "Agitation, nystagmus, hypertension, and possible violent behavior", "choice_b": "Isolated pinpoint pupils", "choice_c": "Predictable, calm cooperation", "choice_d": "Profound sedation and bradycardia", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "PCP typically produces agitation, nystagmus, hypertension, psychosis, hyperthermia, and sometimes violent behavior. Consent capacity may be impaired (Zoppellari et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "A heavy chronic illicit ketamine user may show which additional clinical concern?", "choice_a": "Predictable hypoglycemia", "choice_b": "Chronic urinary tract complications", "choice_c": "Guaranteed cardiomyopathy", "choice_d": "Absence of any tolerance", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Heavy illicit ketamine use is associated with dissociation, tachycardia, hypertension, and chronic urinary complications, and co-use with stimulants or club drugs is common (Zoppellari et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "LSD and psilocybin most typically produce which perioperative concern?", "choice_a": "Profound bradycardia", "choice_b": "Malignant hyperthermia", "choice_c": "Anxiety, panic, perceptual disturbance, and behavioral unpredictability", "choice_d": "Severe cardiorespiratory collapse", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Classic hallucinogens more often create anxiety, panic, perceptual disturbance, and behavioral unpredictability than severe cardiorespiratory toxicity \u2014 reassurance and calm environment are usually most useful (Zoppellari et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "During preoperative screening for illicit drug use, the MOST clinically useful questions include all of the following EXCEPT:", "choice_a": "When it was last used", "choice_b": "How it was used (route)", "choice_c": "What substance was used", "choice_d": "Whether the patient's family morally approves of the use", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Effective preoperative screening focuses on what was used, when, how, how often, current symptoms, and prior complications \u2014 not moral judgment (Gould et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which of the following BEST justifies postponing an elective procedure in a patient with a substance-use history?", "choice_a": "Current acute intoxication, hyperthermia, uncontrolled chest pain, severe agitation, or impaired consent capacity", "choice_b": "Reported use more than one year ago", "choice_c": "Positive urine toxicology alone with normal exam", "choice_d": "Any past use of a controlled substance", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Elective surgery should be postponed for acute intoxication, hyperthermia, chest pain, agitation, or impaired consent capacity \u2014 not for remote use alone (Mishra et al., 2025; Gould et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "A stable patient with a remote history of substance use, no current toxicity, and adequate consent capacity should generally be:", "choice_a": "Refused surgery", "choice_b": "Managed with an anesthetic plan that accounts for tolerance, withdrawal risk, pain needs, and postoperative monitoring", "choice_c": "Fully sedated to \"prevent\" withdrawal", "choice_d": "Treated identically to opioid-na\u00efve patients", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Remote or chronic use without current toxicity is not itself a contraindication. Individualize the plan for tolerance, withdrawal, pain, and monitoring (Gould et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "In the postoperative period, a patient with suspected illicit fentanyl and possible xylazine exposure should ordinarily receive:", "choice_a": "Routine flumazenil administration", "choice_b": "Standard ambulatory discharge criteria", "choice_c": "Extended PACU observation with repeated assessment of sedation, ventilation, and hemodynamics", "choice_d": "Immediate discharge to unmonitored care", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Mixed depressant exposure may cause recurrent hypoventilation and prolonged sedation. Extend observation and reassess repeatedly (Solanki et al., 2024; Hooten, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Which of the following BEST describes the role of multimodal analgesia in patients with chronic illicit opioid exposure?", "choice_a": "Contraindicated in tolerant patients", "choice_b": "Only relevant for opioid-na\u00efve patients", "choice_c": "Optional add-on rarely needed", "choice_d": "Essential \u2014 plan regional techniques, acetaminophen, NSAIDs when safe, and non-opioid adjuncts because opioid-only strategies often fail", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Multimodal analgesia is essential in high-tolerance patients \u2014 pure opioid strategies often fail and postoperative opioid needs are usually higher than expected (Hooten, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "A patient with recent MDMA use presents with hyperthermia, agitation, and hyponatremia. The MOST clinically important perioperative consideration is:", "choice_a": "Address hyperthermia, electrolyte disturbance, and serotonergic effects; consider the risk of dysrhythmia and unpredictable sedative responses", "choice_b": "Immediate elective surgery is safe", "choice_c": "Give routine sympathomimetics to raise BP", "choice_d": "Use spinal anesthesia to bypass all concerns", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "MDMA toxicity may produce hyperthermia, hyponatremia, serotonergic effects, dysrhythmia, and unpredictable sedative responses. Stabilize physiology before elective care.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "A postoperative patient with chronic opioid use develops diaphoresis, mydriasis, restlessness, tachycardia, nausea, and diffuse pain. The BEST assessment is:", "choice_a": "Malingering", "choice_b": "Consider concurrent opioid withdrawal AND under-treated pain, and address both", "choice_c": "Definitely simple PACU delirium requiring physical restraint", "choice_d": "Purely anxiety", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "A patient can be simultaneously opioid-tolerant, under-treated for pain, and starting to withdraw. Treat both and reassess (Hooten, 2024; Regina et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which of the following BEST reflects the modern CRNA approach to illicit drug exposure?", "choice_a": "Apply a universal 30-day abstinence rule regardless of history", "choice_b": "Treat the tox screen result as the entire clinical picture", "choice_c": "Plan around the patient's current toxidrome and physiology, using the drug name and tox screen as one input among several", "choice_d": "Refuse care to any patient with a positive screen", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Modern perioperative care is guided by the patient's current physiology and toxidrome, not a single reported drug name or tox result (Gould et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Which of the following statements about naloxone in the perioperative setting is MOST accurate?", "choice_a": "Naloxone reliably reverses cocaine", "choice_b": "Naloxone reverses opioids, xylazine, GHB, and benzodiazepines", "choice_c": "Naloxone should never be used perioperatively", "choice_d": "Naloxone reverses opioids only; persistent sedation may reflect non-opioid co-exposures", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Naloxone reverses the opioid component, not xylazine. Persistent respiratory depression still warrants airway support and repeat/titrated naloxone when residual opioid effect is possible.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/media/pdfs/2024/07/Xylazine-Clinical-Management-and-Harm-Reduction.pdf"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_8", "question_id": "course_1_module_8_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "A CRNA is planning care for a patient with a known injection-drug-use history and a new heart murmur. Which additional preoperative concern is MOST important?", "choice_a": "Evaluate for infective endocarditis, sepsis, and possible cardiac complications before elective surgery", "choice_b": "Ignore the murmur \u2014 it is unrelated", "choice_c": "Rely on a single normal ECG to exclude cardiac disease", "choice_d": "Cancel all future anesthesia care", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Injection drug use plus a new murmur raises significant concern for infective endocarditis and cardiac complications; evaluate before elective care (Quintana et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.cdc.gov/coca/media/pdfs/2024/022924_transcript.pdf", "https://www.cdc.gov/overdose-prevention/about/what-you-should-know-about-xylazine.html", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39443050/", "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40429-023-00531-1", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38242642/", "https://resources.wfsahq.org/atotw/the-role-of-intoxica"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}], "course_1_module_9": [{"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "MAC is defined as the alveolar concentration at which what percentage of patients do NOT move in response to a surgical incision?", "choice_a": "50%", "choice_b": "25%", "choice_c": "95%", "choice_d": "75%", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "MAC is the alveolar concentration at which 50% of patients do not move in response to a standard surgical incision (Hays, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "Which of the following statements about MAC and age is MOST accurate?", "choice_a": "MAC increases with age", "choice_b": "MAC decreases with age, so older adults typically require less anesthetic", "choice_c": "MAC only decreases in patients over 90", "choice_d": "MAC is independent of age", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "MAC declines progressively with age; older adults generally require less volatile anesthetic than younger adults (Hays, 2025; OpenAnesthesia, 2023).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Which inhaled agent has the LOWEST blood-gas partition coefficient (fastest onset/offset) among commonly used volatile liquids?", "choice_a": "Sevoflurane", "choice_b": "Halothane", "choice_c": "Desflurane", "choice_d": "Isoflurane", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Desflurane has the lowest blood-gas solubility among commonly used volatile liquids, supporting rapid adjustment in anesthetic depth (Essackjee & Sebastian, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "A CRNA plans an inhalational induction in a 4-year-old. Which volatile agent is MOST appropriate?", "choice_a": "Desflurane, because of its rapid onset", "choice_b": "Isoflurane, because of its low cost", "choice_c": "Halothane, because it is the historic standard", "choice_d": "Sevoflurane, because it is nonpungent and well tolerated with a mask", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Sevoflurane is nonpungent, has smooth mask tolerance, and is the agent of choice for pediatric inhalational induction (Hays, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Which finding is MOST characteristic of desflurane compared with sevoflurane?", "choice_a": "Pungent airway irritant; may cause tachycardia and hypertension with rapid increases in inspired concentration", "choice_b": "Nonpungent, ideal for mask induction", "choice_c": "High blood-gas solubility", "choice_d": "No environmental impact", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Desflurane is pungent, can irritate the airway, and rapid increases in inspired concentration can provoke sympathetic stimulation (tachycardia, hypertension) (Essackjee & Sebastian, 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Which of the following is TRUE regarding nitrous oxide potency?", "choice_a": "Its MAC is approximately 1%", "choice_b": "It is a very weak anesthetic (MAC ~104%) but has meaningful analgesic and MAC-sparing properties", "choice_c": "It is equipotent with isoflurane", "choice_d": "It is more potent than sevoflurane", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Nitrous oxide has a MAC of ~104%, making it a weak anesthetic on its own, but it provides analgesia and MAC-sparing effects when combined with volatile agents (Hays, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "A 2024 meta-analysis of 71 RCTs involving 22,147 patients (Kampman et al.) found that adding nitrous oxide to general anesthesia was associated with:", "choice_a": "Reduced postoperative mortality", "choice_b": "No effect on PONV", "choice_c": "Increased PONV without an improvement in major morbidity or mortality", "choice_d": "Reduced overall anesthesia costs", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The 2024 Kampman et al. meta-analysis showed that adding N2O increased PONV (pooled RR 1.27) without improving major postoperative morbidity or mortality (Kampman et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Which of the following is NOT a triggering agent for malignant hyperthermia?", "choice_a": "Desflurane", "choice_b": "Sevoflurane", "choice_c": "Isoflurane", "choice_d": "Nitrous oxide", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Nitrous oxide is NOT a triggering agent for MH. All potent volatile anesthetics (sevoflurane, desflurane, isoflurane) are triggers (Rosenberg et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "Which sign is often the EARLIEST clinical clue of malignant hyperthermia during a volatile-based anesthetic?", "choice_a": "A rapidly rising end-tidal CO2 out of proportion to ventilation, often with tachycardia and rigidity", "choice_b": "Diffuse rash", "choice_c": "Isolated bradycardia", "choice_d": "High fever", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Rapidly rising ETCO2 (often with tachycardia and rigidity) is one of the earliest MH signs; fever is a late sign (Rosenberg et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "A patient is scheduled for retinal surgery with an intraocular gas bubble. Which inhaled agent should be AVOIDED?", "choice_a": "Sevoflurane", "choice_b": "Nitrous oxide", "choice_c": "Isoflurane", "choice_d": "Desflurane", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Nitrous oxide diffuses into closed gas spaces faster than nitrogen can leave, expanding intraocular gas bubbles and threatening vision (Royal College of Ophthalmologists, 2018).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "A CRNA discontinues nitrous oxide at the end of a case. Which action reduces the risk of diffusion hypoxia?", "choice_a": "Immediately extubate on room air", "choice_b": "Increase the volatile agent to compensate", "choice_c": "Administer supplemental oxygen during and after N2O discontinuation", "choice_d": "Continue N2O until extubation", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Rapid N2O washout can dilute alveolar O2 and CO2 \u2014 diffusion hypoxia. Supplemental oxygen during discontinuation is a simple safety step (Abd-Elsayed et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Which inhaled anesthetic has the LARGEST climate impact per case among commonly used volatile agents?", "choice_a": "Sevoflurane", "choice_b": "Isoflurane", "choice_c": "Xenon", "choice_d": "Desflurane", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Desflurane has a disproportionately high greenhouse-gas footprint compared with other commonly used volatile anesthetics (ASA, 2024; Seibold et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "In the 2025 Seibold et al. study of desflurane removal, what happened to average CO2-equivalent emissions per patient over the study period?", "choice_a": "Emissions decreased by roughly 80%, with N2O becoming the largest remaining contributor after desflurane elimination", "choice_b": "Emissions were unchanged", "choice_c": "Only pediatric emissions changed", "choice_d": "Emissions increased modestly", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Mean CO2e per patient fell 82.8% (83.1 kg to 14.3 kg) as desflurane was restricted and eliminated; N2O then became the largest remaining contributor (Seibold et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Which physiologic effect is characteristic of most modern volatile anesthetics when dose is increased?", "choice_a": "Marked increase in tidal volume", "choice_b": "Dose-dependent decrease in blood pressure, primarily through vasodilation", "choice_c": "No effect on ventilation", "choice_d": "Dose-dependent increase in systemic vascular resistance", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "All modern potent volatile agents produce dose-dependent hypotension, primarily via vasodilation and reduced SVR (Hays, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "Which of the following describes the typical respiratory effect of volatile anesthetics?", "choice_a": "Selective stimulation of respiratory rate only", "choice_b": "No effect on respiratory drive", "choice_c": "Dose-dependent ventilatory depression with blunted response to hypercapnia and hypoxia", "choice_d": "Increased tidal volume, enhanced hypercapnic drive", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Volatile anesthetics depress ventilation in a dose-dependent manner, blunt hypercapnic/hypoxic responses, and reduce tidal volume (Hays, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Which neurologic effect is MOST accurate for volatile anesthetics at higher MAC levels?", "choice_a": "No effect on cerebral hemodynamics", "choice_b": "Reduced cerebral blood flow across all doses", "choice_c": "Consistent decrease in ICP", "choice_d": "Reduced cerebral metabolic rate but increased cerebral blood flow, potentially raising ICP in selected patients", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Volatile agents reduce CMRO2 but can increase CBF at higher MAC, which may increase ICP in vulnerable patients (Hays, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "A pediatric patient has a smooth sevoflurane induction but wakes with agitation, kicking, and inconsolability. This is BEST described as:", "choice_a": "Emergence delirium/agitation, a recognized phenomenon after sevoflurane in children", "choice_b": "Laryngospasm", "choice_c": "Malignant hyperthermia", "choice_d": "Anaphylaxis", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Emergence agitation is recognized after pediatric sevoflurane anesthesia, but first assess pain, hypoxemia, airway obstruction, and other reversible causes.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Which patient scenario is the STRONGEST indication to avoid nitrous oxide?", "choice_a": "Healthy adult for hernia repair", "choice_b": "Patient with a known pneumothorax scheduled for urgent surgery", "choice_c": "Patient with well-controlled hypertension", "choice_d": "Adolescent for dental extraction", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Nitrous oxide diffuses into closed gas spaces and can expand a pneumothorax; it should be avoided (Hays, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "Which factor is NOT under the CRNA's direct control when speeding up the onset of an inhaled anesthetic?", "choice_a": "Inspired concentration", "choice_b": "Fresh gas flow", "choice_c": "The blood-gas partition coefficient of the agent", "choice_d": "Alveolar ventilation", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Blood-gas solubility is a fixed property of each agent; inspired concentration, fresh gas flow, and alveolar ventilation are the levers the clinician controls (OpenAnesthesia, 2023).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "A CRNA plans a case for a hemodynamically fragile 80-year-old. Which titration principle is MOST appropriate?", "choice_a": "Use the same end-tidal concentration as for a healthy 30-year-old", "choice_b": "Avoid all volatile anesthetics", "choice_c": "Rely only on BIS without hemodynamic monitoring", "choice_d": "Use age-adjusted MAC and anticipate greater hypotension and slower recovery", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "MAC declines with age; older, frail patients require lower volatile concentrations and are more sensitive to hypotension and delayed recovery (Hays, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Which of the following is the MOST accurate statement about low-flow anesthesia?", "choice_a": "It can reduce volatile consumption and greenhouse gas emissions when used safely in stable patients and circuits", "choice_b": "It reliably increases the environmental footprint", "choice_c": "It is contraindicated in all adult cases", "choice_d": "It requires no attention to inspired oxygen concentration", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Low-flow practice requires oxygen and agent monitoring, suitable equipment and absorbents, and an explicit approach to differing sevoflurane labeling and ASA guidance.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [1, 2], "source_urls": ["https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/drugsatfda_docs/label/2025/020478s035lbl.pdf", "https://www.asahq.org/standards-and-practice-parameters/statement-on-the-use-of-low-gas-flows-for-sevoflurane"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which of the following BEST describes the relationship between end-tidal monitoring and processed EEG (e.g., BIS)?", "choice_a": "Processed EEG is contraindicated with volatile anesthesia", "choice_b": "End-tidal concentration and processed EEG are complementary; BIS may add value in selected patients but does not replace clinical judgment", "choice_c": "End-tidal monitoring is obsolete", "choice_d": "BIS should replace end-tidal monitoring", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Processed EEG complements \u2014 not replaces \u2014 end-tidal monitoring and clinical judgment; it may be helpful when awareness risk or excessive depth is a concern (Rabai et al., 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which statement about the second gas effect is MOST accurate for modern CRNA practice?", "choice_a": "It has been disproven", "choice_b": "It is the dominant factor determining speed of induction today", "choice_c": "It remains conceptually important but its day-to-day clinical impact is usually modest compared with ventilation, fresh gas flow, and overall technique", "choice_d": "It only applies to isoflurane", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The second gas effect is still relevant to understanding gas kinetics but usually has modest day-to-day clinical impact compared with ventilation and flow (Abd-Elsayed et al., 2024).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "Which agent is MOST often described as approaching an 'ideal' inhaled anesthetic profile but is limited by cost and specialized delivery requirements?", "choice_a": "Halothane", "choice_b": "Desflurane", "choice_c": "Isoflurane", "choice_d": "Xenon", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Xenon offers rapid onset/emergence, minimal metabolism, and excellent CV stability but has not been widely adopted due to cost, availability, and the need for closed-circuit delivery systems (Hays, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_9", "question_id": "course_1_module_9_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "A CRNA is emerging a patient from a desflurane-based anesthetic. Which statement BEST reflects modern emergence planning?", "choice_a": "A smooth emergence requires attention to pain, airway reactivity, PONV, neuromuscular recovery, and hemodynamics \u2014 not just choice of a fast agent", "choice_b": "Coughing at emergence is always benign", "choice_c": "Analgesia should never be given before extubation", "choice_d": "Fast emergence is always equivalent to smooth emergence", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Fast wake-up does not guarantee a smooth wake-up. Emergence quality depends on pain, airway, PONV, NMB recovery, and hemodynamics (Hays, 2025).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.asahq.org/about-asa/governance-and-committees/asa-committees/env", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mpaic.2024.09.006", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/inhalation-anesthetic-agents-clinical-effects-and-uses", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2024.08.022", "https://www.uptodate.com/contents/emergence-delirium-and-agitation-in-children", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/inhaled-anesthetic-agents-pharmacodynamics/", "https://www.openanesthesia.org/keywords/eeg-monitoring-and-anesthesia/", "https://www.apsf.org/article/malignant-hyperthermia-moves-out-of-t", "https://www.rcophth.ac.uk/", "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bja.2025.05.031"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}], "course_1_module_10": [{"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which of the following BEST captures the goal of multimodal analgesia in perioperative care?", "choice_a": "Combine agents with different mechanisms of action to improve pain control while reducing opioid-related adverse effects", "choice_b": "Substitute IV acetaminophen for oral acetaminophen in every patient", "choice_c": "Rely on a single high-dose non-opioid agent to avoid drug interactions", "choice_d": "Eliminate all opioid use in every surgical patient", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Multimodal analgesia combines therapies that act at peripheral, spinal, and central levels of pain processing to improve control and reduce opioid requirements \u2014 not to eliminate opioids at all costs.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "Which opioid-related adverse effect is NOT typically reduced by an opioid-sparing multimodal regimen?", "choice_a": "PONV", "choice_b": "Malignant hyperthermia", "choice_c": "Ileus", "choice_d": "Respiratory depression", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Malignant hyperthermia is a pharmacogenetic reaction to volatile anesthetics and succinylcholine \u2014 unrelated to opioid dose. Multimodal analgesia reduces PONV, ileus, sedation, and respiratory depression.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "Recent randomized controlled trial evidence in ambulatory spine surgery (Schwenk et al., 2024) suggests that IV acetaminophen, compared with oral acetaminophen:", "choice_a": "Produces substantially lower pain scores and opioid consumption", "choice_b": "Eliminates the need for NSAIDs postoperatively", "choice_c": "Shows little or no meaningful superiority when oral dosing can be given reliably", "choice_d": "Should always be substituted for oral acetaminophen in ERAS pathways", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Recent RCT evidence shows minimal advantage of IV over oral acetaminophen when PO can be given reliably. IV is best reserved for NPO status, nausea, or unreliable GI absorption.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "The MOST appropriate role for IV acetaminophen in a cost-conscious perioperative practice is:", "choice_a": "Long-term outpatient analgesia after discharge", "choice_b": "Routine substitution for oral acetaminophen in every surgical patient", "choice_c": "First-line therapy for severe postoperative pain", "choice_d": "Selective use in patients who cannot take PO medication or when rapid early dosing is clinically important", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "IV acetaminophen remains substantially more expensive than oral, so selective use is the best-value approach \u2014 reserved for NPO patients, unreliable GI absorption, or when rapid dosing matters.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "For an adult weighing at least 50 kg without hepatic disease or other dose-limiting factors, which is the usual total daily acetaminophen range?", "choice_a": "3\u20134 g/day", "choice_b": "1\u20132 g/day", "choice_c": "8 g/day", "choice_d": "6 g/day", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The usual maximum is up to 4 g/day from all products and routes; many pathways use lower limits. Weight below 50 kg, hepatic risk, and other patient factors require a different limit.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "Which of the following is a specific contraindication to ketorolac?", "choice_a": "Postpartum uterine cramping", "choice_b": "As prophylactic analgesia before major surgery and in the CABG setting", "choice_c": "Ambulatory knee arthroscopy in a healthy adult", "choice_d": "Postoperative outpatient orthopedic surgery in a young adult", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Ketorolac is contraindicated as prophylactic analgesia before major surgery and in the setting of CABG surgery, per current labeling. It is also contraindicated in labor and delivery.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "What is the recommended MAXIMUM total duration of ketorolac use in adults?", "choice_a": "48 hours", "choice_b": "10 days", "choice_c": "5 days", "choice_d": "24 hours", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Even when ketorolac is appropriate, use should be limited to a short course and not continued beyond 5 total days in adults, per prescribing information.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "Which patient is the BEST candidate for ketorolac?", "choice_a": "68-year-old with CKD stage 4 undergoing hip replacement", "choice_b": "45-year-old with active peptic ulcer disease post-cholecystectomy", "choice_c": "60-year-old on aspirin 325 mg daily with recent GI bleed", "choice_d": "32-year-old healthy adult post-laparoscopic appendectomy with good renal function and low bleeding risk", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Advanced renal impairment, active PUD, recent GI bleeding, and concurrent aspirin/NSAID use are all contraindications or strong cautions. A healthy young adult post-laparoscopic surgery is the best candidate.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "In older adults or renal-risk patients when ketorolac is used at all, many clinicians favor which IV dose?", "choice_a": "15 mg IV", "choice_b": "30 mg IV", "choice_c": "60 mg IV", "choice_d": "5 mg IV", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "15 mg IV is commonly favored in older adults or renal-risk patients when ketorolac is used at all, given the higher AKI and bleeding risk profile.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "Which perioperative benefit is BEST supported for a single perioperative dose of dexamethasone in anterior cervical discectomy and fusion (ACDF)?", "choice_a": "Elimination of the need for regional anesthesia", "choice_b": "Reduced postoperative dysphagia and odynophagia", "choice_c": "Improved long-term fusion rate", "choice_d": "Prevention of malignant hyperthermia", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "In ACDF, the literature is particularly supportive of dexamethasone for reducing postoperative dysphagia and odynophagia by limiting soft-tissue inflammatory swelling. Current evidence suggests it does not clearly worsen long-term fusion outcomes.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "The single most important perioperative caution with dexamethasone in a diabetic patient is:", "choice_a": "Hypoglycemia", "choice_b": "Hyperkalemia", "choice_c": "Hyperglycemia", "choice_d": "Bradycardia", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Perioperative hyperglycemia is the most common concern with dexamethasone, particularly in diabetic patients. Single-dose use is broadly accepted but requires glucose monitoring.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "The typical single perioperative IV dexamethasone dose used for antiemetic and analgesic adjunct effect is:", "choice_a": "100 mg", "choice_b": "0.5\u20131 mg", "choice_c": "20\u201340 mg", "choice_d": "4\u201310 mg", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "4\u201310 mg IV is the common single perioperative dose for antiemetic and analgesic adjunct effect in adult surgery.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "Low-dose ketamine's opioid-sparing analgesic effect is primarily mediated by:", "choice_a": "NMDA receptor antagonism reducing central sensitization and wind-up", "choice_b": "Beta-2 adrenergic agonism", "choice_c": "Direct action at spinal mu-opioid receptors", "choice_d": "Inhibition of prostaglandin synthesis", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Ketamine's analgesic action is primarily NMDA receptor antagonism, reducing central sensitization and wind-up \u2014 a mechanism especially useful in major surgery and opioid-tolerant patients.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "Which patient population is MOST likely to benefit from a low-dose ketamine infusion as part of a multimodal plan?", "choice_a": "70-year-old undergoing cataract surgery under topical anesthesia", "choice_b": "Chronic-pain patient on high-dose baseline opioids undergoing major spine surgery", "choice_c": "Healthy 25-year-old undergoing outpatient wrist arthroscopy", "choice_d": "Healthy adult undergoing dental extraction", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Ketamine is particularly useful in major surgery, opioid-tolerant patients, and procedures with high pain burden \u2014 chronic-pain patients on baseline opioids undergoing major spine surgery are a classic indication.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "The MAIN safety concern with perioperative IV lidocaine infusions is:", "choice_a": "Serotonin syndrome", "choice_b": "Adrenal suppression", "choice_c": "Local anesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST), particularly with concurrent regional anesthesia or hepatic dysfunction", "choice_d": "Malignant hyperthermia", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Avoid concurrent or overlapping local-anesthetic effects. The consensus specifies at least 4-hour spacing around nerve blocks, and longer drug action may require more separation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/anae.15270"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "Which IV lidocaine regimen matches the international perioperative consensus ceiling?", "choice_a": "Actual weight: 5 mg/kg rapid bolus", "choice_b": "Combine freely with a simultaneous local-anesthetic nerve block", "choice_c": "Continue 3 mg/kg/h indefinitely", "choice_d": "IBW-based: \u22641.5 mg/kg over 10 min, then \u22641.5 mg/kg/h for \u226424 h with reassessment", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "IV lidocaine is high risk. Use ideal body weight, avoid overlapping local-anesthetic action, and follow the protocol and monitoring requirements.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/anae.15270"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "The MAJOR practical limitation of intraoperative dexmedetomidine infusion is:", "choice_a": "Bradycardia and hypotension", "choice_b": "Tachycardia and hypertension", "choice_c": "Hyperkalemia", "choice_d": "Hyperthermia", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Bradycardia and hypotension are the main practical limitations of dexmedetomidine. Many clinicians reduce or omit the loading dose to limit hemodynamic instability.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Current 2024\u20132025 evidence suggests that perioperative gabapentinoids should be:", "choice_a": "Used routinely in every surgical patient", "choice_b": "Used selectively rather than routinely, with caution in older adults and OSA due to sedation and respiratory risk", "choice_c": "Combined with high-dose opioids for maximum effect", "choice_d": "Reserved only for outpatient cataract surgery", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Current evidence has become more cautious. Gabapentinoids may reduce opioid use in selected settings but increase sedation, dizziness, and respiratory risk \u2014 particularly in older adults, OSA, and when combined with opioids or other sedatives.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "A patient with severe OSA is undergoing major abdominal surgery. Which agent warrants the MOST caution?", "choice_a": "Local wound infiltration with bupivacaine", "choice_b": "A single dose of dexamethasone 8 mg IV", "choice_c": "Oral pregabalin combined with opioids", "choice_d": "Acetaminophen", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Pregabalin in this setting is oral, not IV. Combined sedative and respiratory effects are important in OSA and other high-risk patients.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [2], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Magnesium's adjunctive analgesic mechanism is primarily related to:", "choice_a": "Direct opioid receptor agonism", "choice_b": "Beta-blockade", "choice_c": "COX-1 and COX-2 inhibition", "choice_d": "NMDA-related mechanisms", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Magnesium provides modest analgesic and opioid-sparing benefit through NMDA-related mechanisms. Its role is generally adjunctive rather than foundational.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "At higher exposure levels, a key perioperative concern with magnesium is:", "choice_a": "Potentiation of neuromuscular blockade and hypotension", "choice_b": "Serotonin syndrome", "choice_c": "Bronchoconstriction", "choice_d": "Malignant hyperthermia", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "At higher exposure levels, magnesium potentiates neuromuscular blockade and can cause hypotension and weakness \u2014 important considerations when used alongside NMBAs.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "Which non-opioid strategy is generally considered among the MOST powerful opioid-sparing interventions available to the CRNA?", "choice_a": "IV magnesium bolus without other adjuncts", "choice_b": "Regional and neuraxial techniques (peripheral nerve blocks, spinal, epidural)", "choice_c": "Preoperative high-dose oral acetaminophen alone", "choice_d": "Routine perioperative gabapentin", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Regional and neuraxial techniques directly interrupt pain transmission and are often the single most powerful opioid-sparing intervention \u2014 they should be considered early, not as rescue.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which nonpharmacologic strategy is a valid \u2014 and essentially cost-free \u2014 component of multimodal perioperative pain management?", "choice_a": "Routine deep sedation for 48 hours postoperatively", "choice_b": "Withholding all analgesics to encourage patient tolerance", "choice_c": "Preoperative education, early mobilization, and ice/compression/elevation as appropriate", "choice_d": "Prolonged bed rest to avoid pain amplification", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Preoperative education, early mobilization, and ice/compression/elevation are validated nonpharmacologic adjuncts that reduce pain amplification and improve function \u2014 with essentially no cost.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "A 78-year-old with early CKD (GFR 42) is scheduled for total knee replacement. Which multimodal plan is MOST appropriate?", "choice_a": "IV lidocaine infusion at 5 mg/kg/hr postoperatively without monitoring", "choice_b": "Opioid-only PCA for 48 hours", "choice_c": "Ketorolac 30 mg IV q6h \u00d7 5 days plus high-dose gabapentin 900 mg preop", "choice_d": "Regional block (adductor canal/IPACK), scheduled oral acetaminophen, careful consideration of NSAID risk, avoid routine gabapentinoids, single-dose dexamethasone", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Regional techniques, scheduled acetaminophen, and single-dose dexamethasone are appropriate. NSAIDs (especially ketorolac at 30 mg) warrant caution in CKD, and routine gabapentinoids should be avoided in older adults given sedation and delirium risk.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_10", "question_id": "course_1_module_10_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Which statement BEST summarizes the CRNA approach to non-opioid perioperative pain management?", "choice_a": "The safest strategy is the most appropriate combination for the procedure and the patient, not the largest regimen \u2014 with regional techniques and ERAS pathways guiding the plan", "choice_b": "Opioid PCA should always be first-line for postoperative pain", "choice_c": "IV acetaminophen and gabapentinoids should be given to every patient regardless of comorbidity", "choice_d": "Non-opioid means low-risk, so more agents are always better", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Non-opioid does not automatically mean low-risk. Individualized, procedure-specific, patient-specific planning \u2014 with regional techniques and ERAS pathways as the backbone \u2014 is the current standard.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13439149/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38499358/", "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0972978X24003222", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7367437/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}], "course_1_module_11": [{"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q01", "question_number": 1, "stem": "Which statement BEST reflects the first-principles CRNA approach to acute intraoperative hypertension?", "choice_a": "Identify and correct reversible causes (anesthetic depth, pain, ventilation, bladder, medication withdrawal) before reflexively giving an antihypertensive", "choice_b": "Immediately give an IV antihypertensive to normalize the number on the monitor", "choice_c": "Deepen anesthesia to the point of hypotension whenever pressure rises", "choice_d": "Use hydralazine as the routine first-line agent for any intraoperative hypertension", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Perioperative hypertension is often a physiologic response. Correcting reversible triggers first is safer and often more effective than reflexive drug therapy.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q02", "question_number": 2, "stem": "A patient develops abrupt hypertension immediately after laparoscopic insufflation. Anesthetic depth is adequate and ETCO2 is 46 mm Hg. Which is the MOST appropriate first step?", "choice_a": "Give 20 mg IV labetalol immediately", "choice_b": "Increase minute ventilation, communicate with the surgeon about insufflation pressure, and reassess before pharmacologic therapy", "choice_c": "Start a nitroprusside infusion at 5 mcg/kg/min", "choice_d": "Ignore the pressure until surgery ends", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Insufflation-related hypertension often responds to improved ventilation and lower insufflation pressure. Correct the cause before escalating pharmacologically.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q03", "question_number": 3, "stem": "A 62-year-old with well-controlled asthma has sudden hypertension and tachycardia during laryngoscopy. She is currently wheezing. Which agent is MOST appropriate?", "choice_a": "Labetalol 20 mg IV", "choice_b": "Propranolol 1 mg IV", "choice_c": "Nicardipine or clevidipine", "choice_d": "Esmolol 30 mg IV", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "In active bronchospasm or high asthma risk, a titratable vasodilator such as nicardipine or clevidipine is safer than beta-blockade, which can precipitate bronchospasm.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q04", "question_number": 4, "stem": "Which is the BEST characterization of esmolol\u2019s perioperative role?", "choice_a": "Long-acting alpha-1 antagonist for postoperative hypertension", "choice_b": "Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker for rate control", "choice_c": "Direct arterial vasodilator with unpredictable onset", "choice_d": "Ultra-short-acting beta-1 selective blocker ideal for brief sympathetic surges such as laryngoscopy or emergence when tachycardia is prominent", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Esmolol is a rapidly titratable ultra-short-acting beta-1 selective blocker \u2014 ideal for transient tachycardia-dominant surges.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q05", "question_number": 5, "stem": "Which perioperative IV antihypertensive is MOST likely to be preferred when hypertension is driven by high systemic vascular resistance and heart-rate reduction is undesirable?", "choice_a": "Nicardipine or clevidipine infusion", "choice_b": "Labetalol 20 mg IV", "choice_c": "Diltiazem 0.25 mg/kg IV", "choice_d": "Esmolol infusion", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Nicardipine and clevidipine are dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers that lower SVR without directly slowing heart rate \u2014 matching this physiology.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q06", "question_number": 6, "stem": "A patient with a documented soy and egg allergy needs an infusion for smooth minute-to-minute BP control. Which agent is CONTRAINDICATED?", "choice_a": "Nicardipine", "choice_b": "Clevidipine", "choice_c": "Nitroglycerin", "choice_d": "Esmolol", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Clevidipine is formulated as a lipid emulsion \u2014 contraindicated in soy or egg allergy and defective lipid metabolism.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q07", "question_number": 7, "stem": "For selected patients with controlled hypertension taking ACEi/ARB before elevated-risk noncardiac surgery, current guidance states:", "choice_a": "All indications require a seven-day hold", "choice_b": "All patients must continue regardless of BP", "choice_c": "Omission for 24 hours may reduce intraoperative hypotension", "choice_d": "Omission is mandatory for every procedure", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "The recommendation is conditional, not a universal order; indication, BP, volume status, and risk matter.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q08", "question_number": 8, "stem": "How should an ACE inhibitor prescribed for HFrEF generally be approached perioperatively?", "choice_a": "Continue regardless of shock or severe hyperkalemia", "choice_b": "Replace it automatically with clonidine", "choice_c": "Always stop it for seven days", "choice_d": "Continuation is reasonable unless patient-specific factors require another plan", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "HFrEF differs from uncomplicated hypertension; continuation remains subject to clinical assessment.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q09", "question_number": 9, "stem": "A patient is on chronic metoprolol succinate for hypertension. Which represents the CORRECT preoperative management?", "choice_a": "Continue on the day of surgery \u2014 abrupt withdrawal risks rebound tachycardia, hypertension, and ischemia", "choice_b": "Double the dose on the morning of surgery", "choice_c": "Discontinue on the morning of surgery to prevent bradycardia", "choice_d": "Substitute with clonidine on the day of surgery", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Chronic beta-blockers should be continued through the day of surgery. Abrupt withdrawal risks rebound sympathetic overactivity and ischemic events (2024 ACC/AHA).", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q10", "question_number": 10, "stem": "A preoperative stress test shows ischemia, and the medicine team asks if a beta-blocker should be started before tomorrow\u2019s surgery. Best response per POISE-1 and 2024 guidance:", "choice_a": "Beta-blockers are contraindicated in any patient with ischemia", "choice_b": "Do not initiate a new beta-blocker on the day of surgery \u2014 start at least 8 days preoperatively to assess tolerability and titrate dose", "choice_c": "Start metoprolol at induction to blunt the ischemic response", "choice_d": "Initiate a full dose of atenolol on the morning of surgery", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "POISE-1 showed harm from acute high-dose beta-blocker initiation on the day of surgery. Current guidance is to start at least 8 days preop with titration, or not at all.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q11", "question_number": 11, "stem": "A patient on chronic clonidine misses the morning dose before surgery and presents in PACU with severe hypertension. The MOST likely explanation is:", "choice_a": "Pheochromocytoma", "choice_b": "Volume overload", "choice_c": "Clonidine withdrawal (rebound) hypertension", "choice_d": "New-onset essential hypertension", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Abrupt discontinuation of clonidine (a centrally acting alpha-2 agonist) can produce severe rebound hypertension. The 2024 ACC/AHA guideline supports continuing clonidine through the day of surgery.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [1], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q12", "question_number": 12, "stem": "Which agent is MOST appropriate when perioperative hypertension coexists with active myocardial ischemia and pulmonary congestion?", "choice_a": "Nitroprusside infusion at 5 mcg/kg/min", "choice_b": "Phentolamine bolus", "choice_c": "Hydralazine 10 mg IV", "choice_d": "Nitroglycerin infusion", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Nitroglycerin\u2019s venodilation directly addresses preload, pulmonary congestion, and ischemic physiology \u2014 a better match than isolated arterial vasodilation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q13", "question_number": 13, "stem": "In acute aortic dissection, the anesthetic goal is anti-impulse therapy. Which combination BEST reflects this principle?", "choice_a": "Beta-blockade first (to reduce both HR and shear force), then a vasodilator added if needed", "choice_b": "Phentolamine as the sole agent", "choice_c": "Nitroprusside without heart-rate control", "choice_d": "Vasodilator alone to drop systolic BP as rapidly as possible", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Anti-impulse therapy prioritizes reducing aortic wall stress by controlling both heart rate and blood pressure. Beta-blockade should precede pure vasodilation to prevent reflex tachycardia that increases shear force.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q14", "question_number": 14, "stem": "How should BP goals during TCAR flow reversal be selected?", "choice_a": "Apply one universal systolic target to every patient", "choice_b": "Agree on phase-specific goals with the vascular team based on baseline and cerebral perfusion", "choice_c": "Treat all elevated pressure without considering cerebral perfusion", "choice_d": "Use the same target during every procedural phase", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Institutional ranges are examples, not universal targets. Avoid both hypoperfusion and severe postoperative hypertension.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q15", "question_number": 15, "stem": "A 2024 multicenter comparison of clevidipine and nicardipine in hypertensive crisis found:", "choice_a": "Clevidipine achieved goal blood pressure dramatically faster", "choice_b": "Both drugs were withdrawn from use in ICU care", "choice_c": "Similar time to goal BP and time in range, with significantly lower study-drug volume for clevidipine", "choice_d": "Nicardipine was significantly more effective at BP control", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Johnson et al. reported similar BP control and less study-drug volume with clevidipine, but no difference in total ICU volume. This retrospective study does not establish overall clinical superiority.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [5], "source_urls": ["https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q16", "question_number": 16, "stem": "A patient with pheochromocytoma is undergoing tumor manipulation and has sudden severe hypertension. Which drug is MOST specifically appropriate?", "choice_a": "Metoprolol IV", "choice_b": "Unopposed labetalol IV push", "choice_c": "Diltiazem infusion", "choice_d": "Phentolamine (or a titratable alpha-blocker/vasodilator)", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "In catecholamine excess, alpha-blockade (phentolamine) is appropriate. Unopposed beta-blockade leaves alpha-mediated vasoconstriction unchecked and can worsen hypertension.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q17", "question_number": 17, "stem": "Which agent\u2019s use is typically LIMITED by concerns about overshoot hypotension and cyanide or thiocyanate toxicity with prolonged infusion?", "choice_a": "Nitroprusside", "choice_b": "Esmolol", "choice_c": "Clevidipine", "choice_d": "Nicardipine", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Nitroprusside is potent and rapid-onset but carries cyanide/thiocyanate toxicity risk with prolonged infusion \u2014 reserve for highly monitored settings.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q18", "question_number": 18, "stem": "Which is the MOST accurate description of diltiazem\u2019s role in perioperative hypertension?", "choice_a": "Ultra-short-acting arterial vasodilator equivalent to clevidipine", "choice_b": "Rate-control drug with secondary BP effect \u2014 useful when hypertension coexists with rapid supraventricular arrhythmia; avoid in bradycardia, advanced AV block, or reduced ventricular function", "choice_c": "Contraindicated in all perioperative patients", "choice_d": "First-line agent for isolated SVR-driven hypertension in all patients", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Diltiazem is a non-dihydropyridine CCB \u2014 primarily rate control with secondary BP effect. Reserve for hypertension plus rapid AF/flutter; avoid in bradycardia, advanced AV block, or reduced ventricular function.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q19", "question_number": 19, "stem": "When treating perioperative hypertension in a chronic hypertensive patient, the CORRECT goal is:", "choice_a": "Reduce to systolic 90 mm Hg regardless of baseline", "choice_b": "Bolus a long-acting agent every 5 minutes until pressure is normal", "choice_c": "Controlled, gradual reduction while correcting the underlying cause, avoiding overshoot hypotension that could reduce cerebral, coronary, or renal perfusion", "choice_d": "Rapid normalization of blood pressure within 5 minutes", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Chronic hypertensives have right-shifted autoregulation. Controlled, gradual reduction preserves end-organ perfusion. Aggressive normalization can harm brain, heart, and kidneys.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q20", "question_number": 20, "stem": "Which statement about IV nicardipine in advanced aortic stenosis is correct?", "choice_a": "Labetalol at a reduced dose", "choice_b": "Esmolol titrated slowly", "choice_c": "Careful volume assessment", "choice_d": "Advanced aortic stenosis is a contraindication to IV nicardipine", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "IV nicardipine labeling lists advanced aortic stenosis as a contraindication because afterload reduction can worsen myocardial oxygen balance.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [2], "source_ids": [2], "source_urls": ["https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b0e2bed3-f928-43ab-a05c-275bedc9856f"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q21", "question_number": 21, "stem": "Which pitfall is MOST commonly seen when multiple team members are managing a perioperative hypertensive episode?", "choice_a": "Cumulative dosing from repeated boluses before the first drug has reached peak effect, leading to overshoot hypotension", "choice_b": "Underdosing of ketamine", "choice_c": "Overuse of nitroglycerin in myocardial ischemia", "choice_d": "Failure to give sugammadex", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "Stacked doses from multiple providers before peak effect is a recurring pitfall. Reassess after each intervention.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q22", "question_number": 22, "stem": "A microvascular free-flap reconstruction patient is 3 hours into the case. The BEST hemodynamic goal is:", "choice_a": "No blood pressure monitoring is necessary once the flap is complete", "choice_b": "Stable perfusion pressure \u2014 avoid prolonged hypotension and large pressure swings that could threaten anastomotic flow and flap viability", "choice_c": "Deliberate hypotension to minimize bleeding, regardless of flap perfusion", "choice_d": "Systolic BP <90 mm Hg for the duration of surgery", "correct_choice": "b", "rationale": "Free-flap outcomes depend on adequate driving pressure across the anastomosis. Prolonged hypotension and swings both threaten viability.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["b", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [4], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q23", "question_number": 23, "stem": "Which statement best guides chronic antihypertensive therapy in an older surgical patient?", "choice_a": "Stop all antihypertensives at age 65", "choice_b": "Continue every agent regardless of shock", "choice_c": "Individualize for BP and hypotension risk; age alone does not mandate stopping therapy", "choice_d": "Abruptly stop every chronic beta-blocker", "correct_choice": "c", "rationale": "Assess physiology, indication, conduction status, and adverse effects rather than using age as an automatic stop rule.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [3], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q24", "question_number": 24, "stem": "A PACU patient has persistent BP 195/110 after missing a diuretic. What is the best initial approach?", "choice_a": "Ignore it because a dose was missed", "choice_b": "Rapidly normalize BP with a large long-acting bolus", "choice_c": "Automatically give the missed diuretic without assessing volume or electrolytes", "choice_d": "Confirm BP, assess end-organ symptoms and reversible causes, then individualize treatment", "correct_choice": "d", "rationale": "Evaluate urgency, pain, ventilation, bladder, volume, and medication history. Choose resumption or titratable therapy according to the clinical situation.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["a", "b"], "remaining_choices": ["c", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [1], "source_urls": ["https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213"], "source_provenance": "verified_targeted_update"}, {"module_id": "course_1_module_11", "question_id": "course_1_module_11_q25", "question_number": 25, "stem": "Which statement BEST summarizes the CRNA-focused approach to selecting a perioperative antihypertensive?", "choice_a": "The best agent matches the physiology (heart rate, function, cause), the procedural context, and the patient\u2019s comorbidities \u2014 and short-acting, titratable agents are usually preferred over long-lasting boluses when the pressure may normalize quickly", "choice_b": "One drug fits all patients regardless of comorbidities", "choice_c": "Hydralazine should be first-line for all perioperative hypertension", "choice_d": "The fastest-acting drug is always the safest choice", "correct_choice": "a", "rationale": "The CRNA approach is physiology- and patient-matched with a preference for short-acting titratable agents. This is the guiding principle for safe, effective perioperative BP management.", "hint": {"mode": "eliminate_two_wrong_answers", "eliminate_choices": ["b", "c"], "remaining_choices": ["a", "d"]}, "learning_objective_ids": [5], "source_ids": [], "source_urls": ["https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001285", "https://www.ahajournals.org/doi/10.1161/CIR.0000000000001356", "https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M22-3157", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/10/619", "https://www.ccjm.org/content/92/4/213", "https://kdigo.org/guidelines/ckd-evaluation-and-management/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38923443/", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2515421/", "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18479744/", "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/medicine/articles/10.3389/fmed.2025.1562681/full", "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4278739/"], "source_provenance": "provider_submitted_content_and_reference_list"}]}''',
);
